// Generated by tools/build-content.mjs. Edit the Markdown in content/ instead.
export default {"ai/ai-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Green tests, open door","md":"An assistant wrote an invoice lookup in minutes. It had tests. They passed. The spec said a user must *never* read another user's invoice. No test said so, and the code didn't check. In a multi-tenant SaaS, that is the most expensive kind of bug."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"The whole loop","md":"**Spec** (criteria) → **tests for every criterion** (they fail first) → **AI drafts** → **read the diff** → **tests and tools** → **mutation check** the security rule → **human review** → merge."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Ship it safely","md":"Find what the AI's tests miss, prove it with a test, fix the code, and write down what you'll do differently."}},"ai/claude-code-codex":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The agent that \"fixed\" the build","md":"A team let a coding agent run unattended overnight on a failing build. In the morning CI was green. The agent had deleted four failing tests (\"outdated\"), disabled the pre-commit hook, and force-pushed. Every individual step had a plausible explanation in its log. None of them was what anyone wanted. The tools were fine; the guard rails were missing."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What coding agents are","md":"**Coding agents** (Anthropic's **Claude Code**, OpenAI's **Codex**, and others built into editors) work *in your repository*: they read files, search, edit, run commands and tests, look at the results, and loop until the task is done. They are far more capable than chat, and they act.\n\nThe loop: **read context → plan → edit → run (tests, linters) → read results → repeat → summarise**."},{"id":"model-2","kind":"model","title":"Claude Code basics","md":"- Run `claude` in the repo root. It reads **CLAUDE.md** (project rules, commands, gotchas; `/init` drafts one) and per-folder CLAUDE.md files. SaaS-Foundation's CLAUDE.md is written for both humans and agents.\n- **Plan mode**: the agent investigates and proposes a plan without editing; you approve or correct it first.\n- **Permissions**: by default it asks before edits and commands; you can **allow** safe commands (tests, read-only git) and **deny** dangerous ones in project settings, so the agent can never run them.\n- **Subagents** for focused side tasks (e.g. an independent reviewer), **hooks** that run your own checks on events (e.g. run the formatter after every edit, block certain commands), **MCP servers** to connect tools and data sources, and **skills / slash commands** for repeatable workflows.\n- **Headless mode** (`claude -p \"...\"`) for scripts and CI."},{"id":"model-3","kind":"model","title":"Codex basics","md":"- OpenAI's **Codex** comes as a CLI (`codex`) and as cloud tasks. It reads **AGENTS.md** for project instructions (many tools now read AGENTS.md).\n- It runs with **approval and sandbox modes**, from read-only suggestions through auto-editing to fuller autonomy inside a sandbox."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Rules that apply to every agent","md":"- **Instructions file** up to date (CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md): commands, rules, what never to do.\n- **Least privilege**: allow read-only commands and tests; ask for edits and commits; **deny** `rm -rf`, force-pushes, `--no-verify`, production credentials and database drops.\n- **Work on a branch** (or a separate git worktree), never on `main`.\n- **Read the diff**, not just the summary. Run the tests yourself or check CI.\n- **Small tasks**, frequent check-ins. An agent with a vague goal and no supervision optimises for \"looks done\"."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A very fast junior with power tools","md":"A coding agent is a junior developer who works ten times faster and can use every power tool in the workshop. Brilliant, if you give clear instructions, keep the dangerous tools locked away, and inspect the work. A disaster if you hand over the keys and leave for the weekend."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Put an agent on a leash","md":"A simulated coding agent will implement a validation rule. Read its plan, write a policy that stops the dangerous steps, run it, and review what it actually did."}},"ai/how-llms-work":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The function that didn't exist","md":"An intern asked an assistant how to set a database statement timeout in the ORM. The answer was confident and neatly formatted: call `session.set_timeout(5)`. The intern wrote it, the editor didn't complain, and the code crashed at runtime: there is no such method. The assistant hadn't lied; it had produced the most plausible-sounding text. Knowing *why* that happens is the first step to using these tools well."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What an LLM actually does","md":"- **Tokens**: text is split into pieces (roughly ¾ of a word each). The model reads tokens and predicts the **next token**, again and again.\n- It learned patterns from enormous amounts of text and code. It has **no database of facts** and **no built-in \"I don't know\"**.\n- **Context window**: the text it can consider at once: your prompt, attached files, the conversation, tool results. Anything not in the context, it doesn't know about your project (your versions, your conventions, your other modules).\n- **Temperature and sampling**: outputs are drawn from probabilities, so the same question can give different answers.\n- **Knowledge cutoff**: training data stops at a date; newer libraries and API changes are unknown unless you provide docs.\n- **Tools and agents**: modern assistants can call tools (read files, run commands, search docs). This grounds them in reality, and gives them the power to do damage.\n\n**Hallucinations** are the natural failure mode: plausible APIs, flags, package names, citations or facts that don't exist, stated with full confidence."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A brilliant new colleague with no memory of your company","md":"Imagine a colleague who has read every programming book and every public repository, types at lightning speed and never gets tired, but joined this morning, has never seen your codebase, sometimes fills gaps with confident guesses, and forgets everything when the conversation ends. You'd give them drafts and research. You'd give them context. You'd check their work. You wouldn't give them the production database password."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Prompting basics that actually help","md":"- **Context over cleverness**: paste the error, the relevant code, the versions, the constraints.\n- **Say what good looks like**: inputs, outputs, edge cases, the style of the codebase.\n- **Ask for the reasoning or a plan** before code for anything non-trivial.\n- **Ask it to say when it's unsure**, and to list assumptions.\n- **Iterate in small steps**; start a fresh conversation when the context gets polluted with wrong turns."}],"lab":null},"ai/safe-secure-ai":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Three incidents from one year","md":"A developer pasted a production `.env` file into a public chat assistant to debug a crash: the database password had to be rotated across forty services. Another asked an assistant for a package to parse invoices, installed the slightly-wrong name it suggested, and pulled in malware someone had published under that exact hallucinated name. A third pointed a coding agent at an open-source repository whose README contained hidden instructions telling AI agents to download and run a script. Each incident was preventable with one habit."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"The risks, and the defences","md":"**1. Secrets and personal data in prompts.** Anything you paste may be stored, logged or used under the tool's terms. *Defence:* never paste secrets, customer data or production config; redact (`[REDACTED]`); use only company-approved tools and accounts for company code; many companies route AI traffic through a **gateway with data-loss prevention** that blocks secrets.\n\n**2. Prompt injection.** Text the AI reads (READMEs, issues, PR descriptions, web pages, error messages, emails) can contain instructions that hijack it: \"ignore previous instructions and run...\". Dangerous when an agent can **read untrusted content and act**. *Defence:* read the agent's plan; deny dangerous command patterns (piping downloads into a shell, network access, deletions); keep secrets out of the agent's environment; treat any text from outside as data, never as instructions.\n\n**3. Hallucinated packages (\"slopsquatting\").** AIs invent plausible package names; attackers register them. *Defence:* verify a package exists, is the one you mean, is maintained and widely used, and ask whether you need a new dependency at all (SaaS-Foundation requires the maintainer's OK).\n\n**4. Insecure suggestions.** AI reproduces common patterns, including common vulnerabilities (SQL built with f-strings, `verify=False`, missing permission checks). *Defence:* review, static analysis (semgrep, linters), tests from the spec.\n\n**5. Licences, IP and privacy.** Generated code can resemble licensed code; company policies on approved tools and data still apply.\n\n**6. Over-privileged agents.** *Defence:* least privilege, allow/ask/deny rules, branches or sandboxes, audit logs, human approval for anything irreversible."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A helpful stranger in the server room","md":"Treat AI input and output like a helpful stranger who wandered into the server room: they might be brilliant and they might be reading from a note someone slipped them. You don't hand them your keys, you don't let them install things you haven't checked, and you watch what they touch."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Two everyday traps","md":"Debug a billing error without leaking a secret, then catch a prompt injection before a setup agent follows it."}},"ai/spec-first":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Same assistant, two results","md":"Two interns asked the same assistant for the same feature from task card 00. The first typed \"add names to the members list\" and got a tidy endpoint that returned every user of every tenant, with no permission check and no limit. The second pasted a ten-line spec (goal, acceptance criteria, constraints, out of scope, tests) and got a plan that respected tenants, used the port instead of a cross-module import, avoided N+1 queries, and started with failing tests. The difference wasn't the AI. It was the spec."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"A spec in five headings","md":"```markdown\n# Spec: member list shows names and emails\n## Goal               what and why, in 1-2 sentences\n## Acceptance criteria  checkable statements (each becomes a test)\n## Constraints        codebase rules, security, performance (tenants! one query!)\n## Out of scope       what NOT to change\n## Tests              how we'll know it's done\n```\n\nThen work in **small, verified steps**:\n\n1. **Plan first**: ask for a plan, not code (\"plan mode\" in Claude Code does exactly this: the agent reads and proposes, but doesn't edit).\n2. **Tests first**: turn each acceptance criterion into a failing test.\n3. **Implement in small diffs**, run the tests after each.\n4. **Review** the diff against the spec.\n\nProject instruction files (**CLAUDE.md**, **AGENTS.md**) are a permanent spec: the rules every task must follow. SaaS-Foundation's intern task cards and their skipped spec tests are specs written in advance."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Ordering at a tailor","md":"\"Make me a suit\" gets you *a* suit. Measurements, fabric, occasion, budget and a fitting appointment get you *your* suit. The tailor's skill is the same; the brief decides the result."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps","md":"- Specs that describe the solution but not the acceptance criteria.\n- Forgetting the constraints (tenancy, permissions, performance): the AI can't know them otherwise.\n- One giant prompt for a week of work."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Vague prompt vs spec","md":"Compare what the assistant produces from a one-line wish and from a real spec."}},"ai/verify-ai-output":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The project that reviewed itself","md":"SaaS-Foundation's security milestone was built with heavy AI assistance: careful specs, tests on a real database, mutation checks. Before calling it done, the team ran an **independent review**: a separate AI reviewer that hadn't written the code, given only the code and the threat model, told to break it. It found **four high-severity issues** the author had missed, including password-reset links that could be pointed at an attacker's site by a crafted Host header. All were reproduced, fixed, and covered by regression tests that fail if the fix is removed. The lesson isn't \"AI is bad\"; it's that **the author grading its own work is the weakest check, for humans and AI alike**."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Layers of verification","md":"1. **Tests from the spec**, not from the code: every acceptance criterion gets a test.\n2. **Read the diff**, not the summary.\n3. **Static analysis**: linters, type checks, `semgrep`, secret scanning, dependency audit (all in CI).\n4. **Mutation checks**: break the protection on purpose; a test must fail; restore it.\n5. **Independent review**: a teammate, or a separate AI reviewer briefed with the code and the threat model, not with the author's conclusions.\n6. **CI as the gate**: nothing merges red.\n7. **Small diffs** make every layer work better."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Proofreading your own essay","md":"You read what you *meant* to write. A fresh pair of eyes reads what's actually on the page. AI-written code needs fresh eyes even more, because its mistakes look exactly as polished as its successes."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps","md":"- Asking the same AI \"are you sure?\" (it will often agree with whatever you imply).\n- Accepting \"all tests pass\" without checking whether tests changed.\n- Large AI diffs reviewed in one sitting."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Mutation check by hand","md":"The AI says its validation works and the tests pass. Prove the tests would notice if it didn't."}},"ai/vibe-coding":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The weekend app that couldn't be fixed","md":"A founder \"vibe coded\" a booking app over a weekend: describe a feature, paste the AI's code, run it, paste the error back, repeat, never reading the code. By Sunday it worked, and it was genuinely impressive. Three months and many customers later, a payment bug appeared. Nobody, including the founder, understood how the code worked. Every AI \"fix\" broke something else. They paid a team to rewrite it."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What vibe coding is, and isn't","md":"**Vibe coding** (a term popularised in 2025): building software by prompting an AI and accepting its output based on whether it *seems* to work, without reading or understanding the code.\n\n| Fine for | Dangerous for |\n|---|---|\n| throwaway prototypes and demos | anything with users' data, money or security |\n| exploring \"is this possible?\" | code a team must maintain |\n| personal scripts on your own data | multi-tenant platforms like SaaS-Foundation |\n| learning what an API can do | code you'll be paged about at 3 a.m. |\n\n**The 70% problem**: AI gets you most of the way very fast. The last 30% (edge cases, error handling, security, integration with the rest of the system, performance, maintainability) needs understanding. Vibe-coded projects tend to stall or break exactly there."},{"id":"model-2","kind":"model","title":"AI-assisted engineering","md":"The same tools, used with engineering habits:\n\n1. **You own every line**: if you can't explain it in review, it doesn't merge.\n2. **Spec and context first** (next lesson).\n3. **Small steps**, each verified.\n4. **Tests, static analysis, review**: AI code goes through exactly the same gates as human code, plus extra suspicion in security-sensitive areas.\n5. **Learn from it**: ask *why*, not just *what*. Use AI to understand faster, not to skip understanding."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A calculator in an engineering exam","md":"A calculator makes an engineer faster. It doesn't make someone who doesn't understand the physics into an engineer: they can't tell when the answer is off by a factor of a thousand. AI is the most powerful calculator ever built."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps","md":"- \"It runs, ship it.\"\n- Pasting errors back in a loop without reading them.\n- Accepting new dependencies the AI suggests without checking them.\n- Letting the AI rewrite large areas \"to clean up\" alongside your actual change."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Vibe it, then engineer it","md":"Ask the assistant for a small database function, then review it like an engineer, with a tool to back you up."}},"backend/backend-basics":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The discount that anyone could give themselves","md":"An online shop calculated discounts in the browser and sent the final price to the backend, which saved it. A curious customer opened the browser's DevTools, changed the price to 1 taka, and the order went through. The backend's first job is to never trust the client: it decides prices, permissions and validity itself."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"The backend's jobs, in order","md":"1. **Route** the request to the right handler.\n2. **Authenticate**: who is calling (session, token, API key)?\n3. **Validate** the input: types, required fields, limits.\n4. **Authorise**: may *this* caller do *this* to *this* object, in *this* tenant?\n5. **Apply business rules** and **persist** changes, in one **transaction**.\n6. **Queue side effects** (emails, webhooks) in the same transaction (outbox).\n7. **Respond** with a status and body (or a structured error).\n\n**Layers keep this manageable** (SaaS-Foundation's module layout):\n\n```\nmodels.py      tables (SQLAlchemy)\nrepository.py  queries (tenant-aware, extends BaseRepository)\nservice.py     rules: raises DomainError subclasses, knows nothing about HTTP\nrouter.py      thin: validate, call the service, return a schema\nschemas.py     the API contract (Pydantic)\n```\n\n**Idempotency**: networks fail and clients retry. Operations that must not happen twice (payments, invites) accept an idempotency key or deduplicate."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A bank teller","md":"The teller checks your ID (authentication), checks the form is filled in (validation), checks you own the account (authorisation), applies the bank's rules (no overdraft), records the transaction in the ledger atomically, and only then sends the confirmation letter (side effect). They never let you write your own balance."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps","md":"- Business logic in route handlers (impossible to reuse or test without HTTP).\n- Trusting client-sent prices, roles or tenant ids.\n- Side effects before the transaction commits (an email for an order that rolled back)."}],"lab":null},"backend/backend-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Two alerts on a Monday","md":"Monitoring shows a handful of 500s on `POST /v1/customers` and the customer list page taking around four seconds for one large tenant. Rumana: \"Different problems, probably. Find the cause of each from the evidence, and write fixes I'd approve in review: no 'just restart it'.\""},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"From evidence to fix","md":"1. **Find the failing request** in the logs (status, request id).\n2. **Read the exception** at the bottom of the traceback; connect it to the code.\n3. Ask **\"how could the code's own check be bypassed?\"** (races, case differences).\n4. For slowness: **read the query plan**. *Seq Scan* plus millions of rows removed = missing index.\n5. Fixes come **with tests or verification** (a concurrent-create test; EXPLAIN showing an index scan)."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Diagnose and write the fixes","md":"The incident folder has the API log, the service code and a query plan."}},"backend/background-jobs":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The welcome email for an account that didn't exist","md":"A signup handler sent the welcome email, then saved the user. One day the save failed (a duplicate email): the person got a cheerful welcome email for an account that was never created. Another day the email provider was slow, and signups took 30 seconds. Both bugs disappear with one pattern."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Background work and the outbox","md":"Work that is slow, unreliable or must not be lost runs in the **background**, in a **worker** process, not in the request:\n\n- **Transactional outbox**: write the job to a table in the **same transaction** as the business change. If the change rolls back, the job never existed. If it commits, the job will run.\n- A **worker** claims due jobs (`FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` lets several workers share the queue), runs the handler, and marks them done.\n- **Retries with backoff** (5 s, 10 s, 20 s... capped) for temporary failures; after the maximum attempts, **dead-letter** the job for a human.\n- **At-least-once delivery**: a worker can crash after the work but before marking it done, so the job runs again. Handlers must be **idempotent** (dedupe keys, final states that are never repeated)."},{"id":"inside","kind":"inside","title":"In SaaS-Foundation","md":"```python\nawait notify(app, session, \"auth.password_reset\", to=user.email, data={\"link\": url})\n```\n\n`notify()` writes a delivery row and an outbox job in the caller's transaction. The worker (`make worker`) sends it with the delivery row locked, never re-sends a final delivery, retries 8 times, then dead-letters it, and wipes the data (which may hold the one-time link) once the delivery is final. A concurrency test proves two workers can't send it twice."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A restaurant's order rail","md":"The waiter doesn't cook. They pin the order ticket on the rail (outbox) at the same moment the bill is opened (same transaction). Cooks (workers) take tickets from the rail; if a cook drops a plate, the ticket goes back up (retry); a ticket that keeps failing goes to the head chef (dead letter)."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps","md":"- Side effects inside the request (slow, and wrong on rollback).\n- Non-idempotent handlers (\"charge the card\" without a dedupe key).\n- No limit on retries (a poison job retried forever)."}],"lab":null},"backend/databases-sql":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Four seconds per page","md":"The customer list for one big tenant took four seconds to load. The code was fine; the query was fine. The database was reading all 1.8 million customers of all tenants to find one phone number, because nobody had created an index for that search. One line of migration made it take 2 milliseconds."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Relational databases","md":"- **Tables** of **rows** and typed **columns**. A **primary key** identifies each row (`id`, often a UUID). A **foreign key** references a row in another table.\n- **Constraints** keep data valid even when code is wrong: `NOT NULL`, `UNIQUE (tenant_id, email)`, `CHECK`, foreign keys.\n- **SQL**:\n\n```sql\nSELECT name, email FROM users WHERE tenant_id = $1 AND email = $2;\nINSERT INTO notes (id, tenant_id, title) VALUES ($1, $2, $3);\nUPDATE notes SET done = true WHERE id = $1;   -- always a WHERE!\nDELETE FROM notes WHERE id = $1;\nSELECT c.name, o.id FROM customers c JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id;  -- inner join: matches only\n```\n\n- **Indexes** make lookups fast (like a book's index) at the cost of slower writes and some space. **`EXPLAIN ANALYZE`** shows the plan: a *Seq Scan* over millions of rows means a missing index.\n- **Transactions**: a group of statements that succeed or fail together (**ACID**: atomic, consistent, isolated, durable).\n- **Race conditions**: \"check then insert\" can race; a `UNIQUE` constraint can't."},{"id":"inside","kind":"inside","title":"PostgreSQL in SaaS-Foundation","md":"- Every tenant table has a `tenant_id` and **row-level security**: the database itself filters every query to the current tenant (Realm 8 explains how).\n- Modules don't use foreign keys *into other modules' tables* (plain UUIDs keep modules independent).\n- Tests run on a **real PostgreSQL**, because the security lives in the database."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A library card catalogue","md":"Tables are shelves of identical index cards. A primary key is each card's number. An index is the catalogue sorted by author: finding all books by one author takes seconds instead of reading every card in the library."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps","md":"- `UPDATE`/`DELETE` without `WHERE`.\n- Building SQL with string concatenation (injection).\n- Lists without `LIMIT`.\n- Missing indexes on columns you filter or join by."}],"lab":null},"backend/fastapi":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Documentation that can't go stale","md":"Most API documentation drifts from the code within months. In SaaS-Foundation it can't: FastAPI builds the OpenAPI description from the Python types of each route, the TypeScript SDK is generated from that, and CI fails if the committed SDK doesn't match. Change a field in Python, and the frontend's types change with it."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"FastAPI in one example","md":"```python\nrouter = APIRouter()\n\n@router.post(\"/{org_id}/invites\", response_model=InviteOut, status_code=201)\nasync def create_invite(\n    org_id: uuid.UUID,                                              # path parameter, validated as a UUID\n    data: InviteIn,                                                 # JSON body, validated by Pydantic\n    request: Request,\n    principal: Principal = Depends(require(\"organizations.invite\")),  # 401/403 before the body runs\n    session: AsyncSession = Depends(get_session),                   # a session bound to this tenant (RLS)\n) -> InviteOut:\n    invite = await invites.create(request.app, session, principal, org_id, str(data.email), data.role, ...)\n    return InviteOut.model_validate(invite, from_attributes=True)\n```\n\n- **Path operations**: a decorator per method and path.\n- **Validation**: parameters and bodies are checked against their types; failures return **422** with a `detail` list whose `loc` points at the exact field (`[\"body\", \"items\", 0, \"qty\"]`).\n- **Dependencies** (`Depends`): reusable pieces a route needs (the DB session, the current user, a permission check). They run first and can stop the request.\n- **`response_model`**: filters the output, so internal fields never leak, and documents it.\n- **OpenAPI** at `/openapi.json` and interactive docs at `/docs`."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A building's reception desk","md":"Dependencies are the security gates you pass before reaching an office: badge check (authentication), floor permission (authorisation), a visitor log (session). Pydantic is the form at reception that won't accept \"tomorrow-ish\" as a date. The office (your handler) only ever sees checked people with valid forms."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps","md":"- Returning ORM objects without a response model (password hashes and internal ids leak).\n- Doing permission checks inside the function body inconsistently instead of with `require(...)`.\n- Validation in the frontend only."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Talk to a FastAPI service","md":"A FastAPI orders service is running. Read its self-description, then trigger its validation and read the errors the way a frontend developer would."}},"backend/orm-migrations":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"\"It works on my database\"","md":"A developer added a column to a model, tested locally (where the table was recreated from scratch), and merged. Production's table, which had two years of data, still had the old shape: the deploy crashed. Since then the team has two rules: every model change ships with a **migration**, and a test fails if models and migrations disagree."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"SQLAlchemy and Alembic","md":"**SQLAlchemy** (the ORM) maps tables to Python classes and builds parameterised SQL:\n\n```python\nclass Note(Base, TenantScoped, Timestamped):\n    __tablename__ = \"notes\"\n    id: Mapped[uuid.UUID] = mapped_column(primary_key=True, default=uuid.uuid4)\n    title: Mapped[str] = mapped_column(String(200))\n\nstmt = select(Note).where(Note.title.ilike(\"%flour%\")).limit(50)\nrows = (await session.execute(stmt)).scalars().all()\n```\n\n**Alembic** migrations change the schema step by step, the same way everywhere:\n\n```python\nrevision = \"notes_0002\"; down_revision = \"notes_0001\"\ndef upgrade():\n    op.add_column(\"notes\", sa.Column(\"done\", sa.Boolean(), server_default=sa.false(), nullable=False))\n```"},{"id":"inside","kind":"inside","title":"SaaS-Foundation's migration rules","md":"- Each module has its **own Alembic branch** (`branch_labels = (\"notes\",)`), so a product gets exactly its modules' tables.\n- Every tenant table's first migration calls **`enable_rls(\"<table>\")`**: a test fails otherwise.\n- A **drift test** compares models with migrations; an **upgrade test** builds a database at each module's *first* revision, seeds data, upgrades to the latest and checks the data survived. New NOT NULL columns without a `server_default` fail it.\n- **Never edit a merged migration**: write a new one."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Renovation plans","md":"Models are the architect's drawing of how the house should look. Migrations are the numbered work orders that get every real house (your laptop, CI, staging, production) from its current state to the drawing, without demolishing it."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps","md":"- The **N+1 query**: a loop that runs one query per row. Load related rows in one query.\n- Editing old migrations.\n- NOT NULL columns without defaults on existing tables.\n- Assuming the ORM makes queries cheap: read the SQL it generates when performance matters."}],"lab":null},"backend/python":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The list that remembered","md":"An intern wrote `def add_tag(tag, tags=[])` and couldn't understand why every new note \"inherited\" tags from previous ones. Python creates default values once, when the function is defined. Five minutes with a mentor, and a lesson remembered for life. Every language has a handful of these; here are Python's that matter in SaaS-Foundation."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"The Python you need here","md":"- **Type hints** everywhere: `def total(amounts: list[int]) -> int:`. Python ignores them at runtime, but editors, type checkers, **Pydantic** and **FastAPI** read them to validate data and generate docs.\n- **Pydantic models** describe data shapes with validation:\n\n```python\nclass InviteIn(BaseModel):\n    email: EmailStr\n    role: str = Field(default=\"member\", max_length=32)\n```\n\n- **Dataclasses** for simple internal value objects (`@dataclass(frozen=True)`).\n- **Exceptions**: raise specific ones (`Invalid`, `NotFound`, `Conflict` from `foundation_core`), catch only what you can handle.\n- **Context managers** (`with` / `async with`) guarantee cleanup: transactions commit or roll back, connections return to the pool.\n- **async/await**: the API is asynchronous. Awaiting I/O (database, HTTP) lets other requests run meanwhile. CPU-heavy work (password hashing) blocks the loop, so SaaS-Foundation runs it in a thread (`asyncio.to_thread`).\n- **Tooling**: `uv` manages the virtual environment and the lockfile; `make setup`, `make test`, `make check`."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A chef's knives","md":"You don't need every utensil in the shop: you need a few knives, kept sharp, and to know which one is dangerous in which way. Type hints, Pydantic, context managers and async are this codebase's knives."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps","md":"- Mutable default arguments.\n- Blocking calls (`time.sleep`, `requests.get`, heavy CPU) inside async code.\n- `except Exception: pass`.\n- Forgetting `await` (you get a coroutine object, not a result)."}],"lab":null},"basecamp/basecamp-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The smallest real change","md":"Rumana gives you ticket FND-142: a typo in a welcome email. \"It's tiny on purpose,\" she says. \"I don't care about the typo. I care that you find the right file, change only what the ticket asks, check you didn't break anything, and tell me clearly what you did.\""},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"The anatomy of a good small change","md":"1. **Read the ticket** completely, especially the acceptance criteria.\n2. **Locate** the code by searching (`grep -rn \"text\" .`), not by guessing.\n3. **Branch** first: `main` stays clean.\n4. Make the **smallest change** that meets the criteria. Stay in scope.\n5. **Verify** against each acceptance criterion.\n6. **Commit** with a message that says what and why, and names the ticket.\n7. **Report**: what changed, where, how you checked it, anything left open."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Ticket FND-142","md":"Work through the ticket the way the team does. Every step is something you will do on real tickets in the SaaS-Foundation repo."}},"basecamp/how-teams-ship":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"From \"wouldn't it be nice\" to production","md":"Support gets an email: an Acme Bakery admin wants to see which emails bounced. A product manager writes a **ticket** with the problem and **acceptance criteria**. In planning, the team agrees it fits this sprint. Tanvir picks it up, creates a **branch**, writes the code and the **tests**, and opens a **draft pull request** on day one so Rumana can see the direction. **CI** runs 237 tests and a dozen checks on every push. Rumana **reviews**, asks for one change, approves. The PR is **merged**, the pipeline **deploys** it, and the support team tells the customer. Elapsed time: three days. Nobody worked late."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"The delivery loop","md":"```\nidea → ticket → branch → small commits + tests → pull request → CI checks → review → merge → deploy → monitor\n                                                       ↑                                              │\n                                                       └──────────────── feedback ────────────────────┘\n```\n\n- **Ticket**: describes the problem, not just the solution, and how we will know it is done (acceptance criteria).\n- **Branch**: your change lives apart from `main` until it is ready, so `main` stays releasable.\n- **Pull request (PR)**: a request to merge your branch, with a description, a diff, CI results and review comments. It is also the permanent record of *why* the change happened.\n- **CI (continuous integration)**: robots that run the tests, linters, type checks and security scans on every push. Red CI = do not merge.\n- **Review**: a teammate reads the change. Two people understand every line that ships.\n- **Deploy**: automated, frequent and boring. Small changes, deployed often, are *safer* than big releases."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A restaurant kitchen","md":"The ticket is the order slip. The branch is your own station. The pull request is the plate you put on the pass. CI is the checklist (temperature, allergens, presentation), and the reviewer is the head chef who looks before it goes out. Nobody carries food straight from a cutting board to a customer."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Agile, sprints and boards","md":"Most teams organise this loop with an **agile** method:\n\n- **Scrum**: fixed **sprints** (often two weeks), planning at the start, a daily stand-up (\"what I did, what I'll do, what blocks me\"), a demo and a **retrospective** at the end.\n- **Kanban**: a continuous flow on a board (To do → In progress → Review → Done) with **work-in-progress limits** so things get *finished* instead of started.\n\nEither way, the ideas are the same: **small batches, visible work, fast feedback, and a shared definition of done** (tests pass, reviewed, documented, deployed)."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Where changes get stuck","md":"- Tickets with a solution but no problem (\"add a button\") instead of \"admins can't see bounced emails\".\n- Branches that live for weeks and then conflict with everything.\n- PRs with no description: the reviewer has to reverse-engineer your intent.\n- Skipping CI \"just this once\". The one time is always the time it mattered."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Look at a real history","md":"Every project carries its history in git. Before changing anything, look at what happened before and how the team wants changes made. Then start your work on a branch."}},"basecamp/learning-fast":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Three hours vs ten minutes","md":"Two interns hit the same error on their first day: the test database would not start. Intern A spent three hours trying random fixes found online, reinstalled Python twice, and went home frustrated. Intern B tried for twenty minutes, then wrote in the team chat:\n\n> I'm running `make test` on a fresh clone. It fails with `ConnectionRefusedError` on port 5432. I've run `make setup`; Postgres isn't installed locally. I expected the tests to start their own database (the docs say \"no setup needed\"). Am I missing a step, or is it something on my machine?\n\nRumana answered in two minutes: an old `.env` file was pointing the tests at a real database. Fixed in ten minutes total."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"How to learn a codebase (and anything else) fast","md":"**Read in this order**: README → contributing guide → architecture notes → the tests for the area you are touching → the code itself. Tests are executable examples of how the code is meant to be used.\n\n**Time-box.** Pick a limit (20-30 minutes). When you reach it, stop and ask, with context.\n\n**Ask a question that can be answered quickly:**\n\n1. **Goal**: what are you trying to achieve? (Avoid the *XY problem*: asking about your attempted fix instead of your real goal.)\n2. **What you did**: commands, with the **exact error text** (copy and paste text, not just a screenshot).\n3. **Expected vs actual**.\n4. **Your best guess** at the cause, even if it is wrong.\n\n**Rubber-duck debugging**: explain the problem out loud, step by step, to anyone or anything. Very often you find the mistake halfway through the explanation.\n\n**Keep notes**: commands that worked, things you learned, questions to ask later. Your future self is your most frequent reader."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Learning a new city","md":"You do not memorise every street before your first day. You learn the main roads (the architecture), find your own neighbourhood (the module you work on), ask locals for directions (teammates), and keep a map with your own notes. Within weeks you are giving directions to newer people."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"What mentors actually want","md":"- Questions **early**, with context. Silent struggling is the thing that worries them.\n- **Visible progress**: draft PRs, short updates, notes.\n- **Curiosity about why**, not just how: \"why do we use row-level security here?\" teaches you more than \"where do I put this line?\".\n- **Following up**: when you solve something, write it down (or improve the docs) so the next intern doesn't hit it."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Learning traps","md":"- **Tutorial hell**: watching and reading without ever building. Learn by changing real code, in small steps.\n- **Copy-paste without understanding** (from the internet *or* from AI). If you can't explain a line, you can't own it in review.\n- **Fixing symptoms**: the test passes now, but you don't know why it failed. Find the cause."}],"lab":null},"basecamp/welcome":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Day one","md":"Nadia joined the platform team on a Sunday morning. She had built a to-do app in university and won a hackathon. By lunch she had learned that the codebase had 40,000 lines she had never seen, that her first task was to fix one word in an email, and that the fix would need a branch, a test run, a pull request, a reviewer and a deploy.\n\n\"So most of the job is *not* writing code?\" she asked her mentor, Rumana.\n\n\"Most of the job is understanding code, changing it safely, and making sure other people can keep changing it after you,\" Rumana said. \"Writing the code is the fun bit at the end.\""},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Programming vs software engineering","md":"**Programming** is telling a computer what to do. **Software engineering** is programming *over time, with other people, for users who depend on the result*.\n\n| Programming asks | Engineering also asks |\n|---|---|\n| Does it work? | Will it still work after 50 other changes? |\n| Is it fast enough? | Can a teammate understand and change it next year? |\n| Does it run on my machine? | Does it run the same in production, for 10,000 users? |\n| | What happens when it fails? Who notices? How do we recover? |\n| | Is it secure? Is user data safe? |\n\nThat is why engineers care so much about **readability, tests, reviews, small changes, documentation and automation**: they are the tools that let many people change a system for years without breaking it.\n\nWhat you will actually do as an intern:\n\n- **Read** a lot of code and docs (far more than you write).\n- **Ask** good questions, early.\n- Make **small, reviewed changes** with tests.\n- **Leave a trail**: commit messages, PR descriptions, notes that the next person can follow."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Building in an occupied house","md":"Writing a program from scratch is like building a shed in an empty field. Professional software engineering is renovating a house *while the family lives in it*: the water must keep running, nobody can be hurt, every change must be planned and inspected, and the next builder must be able to understand what you did from the drawings you left behind."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"How this course works","md":"This course is a **conquest**: realms of lessons on a map, from Base Camp to the Hero Trials.\n\n- Each lesson has three stages: **Learn** (read every lens, like this one), **Practice** (a hands-on lab in a simulated terminal, when the lesson has one), **Prove** (a quiz; 80% to pass).\n- Lessons unlock in order: mastery first, so every new idea sits on solid ground.\n- You earn **XP** and climb **ranks**. Perfect quizzes earn bonus XP. Each realm ends with a **Boss** lesson.\n- Everything runs in your browser. The terminal and servers are simulated, so you cannot break anything real. Experiment."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps for new engineers","md":"- **Staying stuck in silence.** After 20-30 minutes without progress, write down what you tried and ask.\n- **Rewriting instead of reading.** The existing code usually has reasons. Find them (tests, git history, docs) before changing it.\n- **Big-bang changes.** One huge pull request is hard to review and risky to ship. Small steps win.\n- **\"It works on my machine.\"** Your machine is not production. Tests and CI are the shared truth."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Your first commands","md":"The terminal is a text interface to the computer: you type a command, press Enter, and read the answer. You will use it every day: to run the project, run tests, use git, and talk to servers.\n\nTry these, and read the output each time. Nothing here can break anything."}},"craft/clean-code":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Code is read far more than it is written","md":"A function written in 20 minutes gets read hundreds of times: by reviewers, by the next person fixing a bug at 2 a.m., by you in six months when you have forgotten everything. Rumana's rule for interns: **\"If I need you to explain it, the code isn't finished.\"**"},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What clean code looks like","md":"**Names that explain.** `reset_link_ttl_seconds` beats `t`. Booleans read like questions: `is_verified`, `has_mfa`. Avoid double negatives (`is_not_disabled`).\n\n**Small functions that do one thing.** If the name needs \"and\" (`validate_and_save_and_email`), split it. Small functions are easier to test.\n\n**No magic numbers.** `if retries > MAX_RETRIES` instead of `if x[2] > 3`.\n\n**Comments say why, not what.** The code says what it does; a comment explains a constraint, a decision, or a trap:\n\n```python\n# Reset links expire after 30 min: long enough for slow email, short enough\n# that an old inbox can't be used to take over the account.\nRESET_TTL = timedelta(minutes=30)\n```\n\n**Consistent formatting**, enforced by tools (formatters, linters), not by debates in review.\n\n**Errors that help.** \"Use at least 12 characters\" beats \"invalid input\"."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Writing for a stranger","md":"Imagine your code will be maintained by a capable stranger who has no way to ask you questions. Everything they need must be on the page: clear names, obvious structure, and a note wherever something surprising is going on. In a few months, that stranger is you."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Principles you will hear in reviews","md":"- **KISS** (keep it simple): the simplest thing that works is easiest to change.\n- **DRY** (don't repeat yourself): one source of truth for each rule, but don't merge things that only *look* alike.\n- **YAGNI** (you aren't gonna need it): don't build for imagined future requirements.\n- **Boy-scout rule**: leave the code a little cleaner than you found it, in small safe steps that don't derail your ticket."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Clean-code traps","md":"- **Clever one-liners** that save three lines and cost every reader five minutes.\n- **Comments that lie**: the code changed, the comment didn't. Prefer names over comments where possible.\n- **Premature abstraction**: a \"generic framework\" for something used once.\n- **Refactoring and changing behaviour in the same commit**: reviewers can't tell which is which."}],"lab":null},"craft/craft-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Seven commits, one silent bug","md":"Customers report that members are getting 20% off instead of 10%. There are seven commits since pricing was last known to be right, most of them \"harmless\" changelog edits and a refactor. Rumana: \"Don't read every diff. Let git do the search.\""},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"git bisect","md":"`git bisect` is a **binary search** through history: you tell it one good and one bad commit, it checks out the one in the middle, you (or a script) say good or bad, and it halves the range again. 1,000 commits need about 10 checks.\n\n```\ngit bisect start <bad> <good>\ngit bisect run ./check.sh      # exit code 0 = good, anything else = bad\ngit bisect reset               # back to where you were\n```\n\nThen **fix forward**: a new commit (or `git revert <id>`) on top of shared history. Never rewrite commits other people already have."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Find the commit, fix it forward","md":"You have a script that knows the right answer (`check.sh`) and a history that broke it somewhere. Combine them."}},"craft/debugging":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"\"It just broke\"","md":"Orders started failing at 09:15 on a Sunday. Nothing had been deployed, people said. Ten minutes later the cause was found, not by guessing, but by reading: the log said `could not convert string to float: '15%'`, the setting it came from was `TAX_RATE`, and `git log -S \"15%\"` showed a config commit from Thursday titled \"Make tax rate easier to read\". Easier to read for humans, impossible to read for the code."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"The scientific method for bugs","md":"1. **Reproduce** it reliably. If you can't trigger it, you can't know you fixed it.\n2. **Read the evidence**: the exact error, the logs, the stack trace, *what changed recently* (code, config, data, dependencies).\n3. **Form one hypothesis** (\"TAX_RATE is not a number\").\n4. **Test it**: one change or one check at a time.\n5. **Fix the cause**, not the symptom, and **add a test** (or a check) so it can't silently come back.\n\n**Reading a Python traceback**: start at the **bottom** (exception type and message), then walk **up** until you reach a line of *your* code: that is usually where to look.\n\n**Finding the change that caused it:**\n\n- `git log -S \"text\"`: commits that added or removed a string.\n- `git log -p path` / `git blame file`: the history of a file, line by line.\n- `git bisect`: binary search through history with a test (next lesson's boss)."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A detective, not a fortune teller","md":"A detective doesn't announce the culprit after looking at the room for two seconds. They collect evidence, form a theory, look for the one clue that would prove it wrong, and only then make an arrest. Guess-and-check debugging is fortune telling with extra steps."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Debugging on a team","md":"- Say what you've ruled out when you ask for help: it halves the time.\n- Write down the timeline while you investigate; it becomes the incident note.\n- **Blameless**: the question is \"what allowed this?\" (no validation on the config value, no test) rather than \"who did this?\"."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Debugging traps","md":"- Changing many things at once.\n- Fixing the symptom (catching the exception and returning 0% tax) instead of the cause.\n- Ignoring the first error in a log and chasing the last one (later errors are often consequences).\n- \"It works now\" without knowing why."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"A 500 on every new order","md":"The orders service returns 500 for new orders. Work from the evidence to the cause, find the change that introduced it, and fix it."}},"craft/terminal-git":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The intern who lost a day of work","md":"Farhan spent a whole day improving the signup form. In the evening he tried a command from a forum to \"clean up\" his folder and deleted every change. There was no undo: he had never committed. The next morning Rumana showed him the habit that would have saved him: **commit small, commit often, push your branch**. A commit is a save point you can always return to."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"The terminal in five minutes","md":"The **terminal** (shell) runs commands in a **current folder** (`pwd` shows it).\n\n| Command | Does |\n|---|---|\n| `pwd` / `ls -la` / `cd folder` / `cd ..` | where am I / what is here (with hidden files) / go in / go up |\n| `cat file` / `less file` / `head` / `tail` | show a file / page through it / first lines / last lines |\n| `grep -rn \"text\" .` | search every file below here, with line numbers |\n| `mkdir -p a/b` / `cp` / `mv` / `rm` | make folders / copy / move or rename / delete (no recycle bin!) |\n| `echo \"hi\" > f` / `>> f` | write (overwrite) / append |\n| `cmd1 \\| cmd2` | pipe: the output of one command becomes the input of the next |\n\nTab completes names; the Up arrow recalls previous commands; Ctrl+C stops a running command."},{"id":"model-2","kind":"model","title":"Git: the save points of a team","md":"**Git** records snapshots (**commits**) of a project. Each commit has an id, an author, a message and a parent: together they form the history.\n\nThree places your changes can be:\n\n```\nworking folder  --git add-->  staging area  --git commit-->  history  --git push-->  GitHub\n```\n\nThe everyday loop: `git status` → `git diff` → `git add <files>` → `git commit -m \"message\"` → `git push`.\n\n**Branches** are movable labels on commits. You do your work on a branch (`git switch -c my-change`), and `main` stays clean until your pull request is merged."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A lab notebook","md":"Scientists write every experiment in a numbered, dated notebook and never tear pages out. A git history is the team's lab notebook: what changed, who changed it, and (in the message) **why**. `git status` is reading your current page before you write on it."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Git traps","md":"- Committing everything with `git add .` without looking: you commit debug prints, `.env` files with secrets, or huge files. Look with `git status` and `git diff` first.\n- Messages like \"fix\" or \"wip\". Write what and why: \"Fix tax rounding for BDT amounts\".\n- Working on `main` directly. Always branch.\n- `rm` and `git reset --hard` are not undoable for uncommitted work. Commit first."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"The everyday loop","md":"Practise the loop you will run dozens of times a day: look, change, look again, stage, commit, review the history."}},"craft/testing":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The test that saved a Friday","md":"Tanvir changed how discounts round. The change looked harmless and the app ran fine. CI failed: a test called `test_50_items_get_20_percent` now returned 19.99. The bug never reached a customer. Without that test, the first report would have come from an angry accountant at month end."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Why and how we test","md":"Tests let you **change code without fear**. They are also executable documentation: the best example of how code is meant to be used.\n\n**The test pyramid:**\n\n- **Unit tests** (many): one function or class, milliseconds each.\n- **Integration tests** (some): several parts together, e.g. an API route with a real database. SaaS-Foundation runs most of its tests this way, on a real Postgres, because the security (row-level security) lives in the database.\n- **End-to-end tests** (few): the whole system through the UI or API.\n\n**Arrange, Act, Assert**: set up the situation, do one thing, check the result. One behaviour per test, with a name that says it: `test_expired_reset_link_is_rejected`.\n\n**Edge cases** are where bugs live: boundaries (9 vs 10 items), zero, empty, negative, very large, missing, unicode, concurrent access.\n\n**Test-first (TDD)**: write a failing test that describes the behaviour, write the simplest code that passes, then clean up. Red, green, refactor."},{"id":"inside","kind":"inside","title":"Mutation checks: testing the tests","md":"A test that never fails proves nothing. In SaaS-Foundation, every security rule gets a **mutation check**: break the protection on purpose (delete the line that checks the token's expiry), run the tests, and make sure at least one fails. If none fail, the test suite has a hole. You will see this in every PR description of the project."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Smoke detectors","md":"Tests are smoke detectors: cheap to install, silent when everything is fine, loud the moment something burns. A detector with no battery (a test that cannot fail) is worse than none: it makes you feel safe."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Testing traps","md":"- Testing only the happy path.\n- Tests that depend on each other's order, the time of day, or the network: they become **flaky**, and people learn to ignore red.\n- Asserting too little (`assert response` passes for any non-empty response).\n- Mocking so much that the test only tests the mocks."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Tests at the boundaries","md":"A tiny shop gives 10% off for 10+ items and 20% for 50+. The tests pass, but they never check the boundaries where off-by-one bugs live. Make them stronger."}},"foundation/contributing":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The intern who shipped in week one","md":"Two interns started together. One worked alone for two weeks on a big change and opened a 1,500-line PR that needed a week of review and rework. The other picked task card 00, opened a draft PR on day two with a plan and a failing test, got two comments, and merged a small, solid change by Thursday. Both learned; one learned much faster."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"The workflow","md":"1. **Pick the card** (in order, or as assigned). Read it and the files it lists.\n2. **Branch**: `git switch -c m2-<card>-<short-name>`.\n3. **Spec tests first**: move the card's file from `apps/api/tests/todo/` next to the other tests, remove the `skip`, and make them pass, adding cases you discover.\n4. **Draft PR early**, with the template's done checklist.\n5. **`make check`**; for security rules, **mutation checks** recorded in the PR.\n6. **Review**: answer every comment. Cards marked **Security review** wait for the maintainer.\n7. **The maintainer merges.** Interns never merge their own work.\n\n**Ask before:** adding a dependency, changing `foundation_core`, editing a merged migration, weakening a test, touching auth or MFA."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Climbing with a partner","md":"You choose a route (task card), plan it at the bottom (draft PR with a plan), climb in short pitches with protection placed often (small commits and tests), and your partner checks every knot before you rely on it (review)."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps","md":"- Going silent for days.\n- PRs that mix the task with unrelated clean-ups.\n- \"I'll add tests at the end.\""}],"lab":null},"foundation/foundation-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Plan before you type","md":"Rumana: \"Before you write a line for card 00, show me a plan: every file you'll touch, how you'll keep it to one query, and which tests prove it. If the plan is right, the code takes an hour.\""},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"A good change plan","md":"- **Files** to change, and why each.\n- **Rules** it must respect (no cross-module imports, no N+1 queries, permissions).\n- **Tests** that will prove it (including the cross-tenant case).\n- **Done**: `make sdk`, `make check`, draft PR."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Investigate task 00","md":"Find everything the change touches in the miniature codebase, branch, and write the plan."}},"foundation/module-anatomy":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Swapping an email provider in one PR","md":"When a product needed a new email channel, the change touched one module. Auth, invites and every other sender kept calling the same `notify()` port; none of them knew how email worked. That's the payoff of a module that is complete, self-contained and connected through ports."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What's inside a module","md":"```\nmodules/notifications/src/foundation_notifications/\n  __init__.py      the manifest: define_module(name, depends_on, permissions, tenant_grants,\n                   models, migrations, templates, settings, owner_only, setup, ...)\n  models.py        tables (TenantScoped for tenant data)\n  migrations/versions/0001_initial.py   its own Alembic branch; enable_rls(...) for tenant tables\n  schemas.py       Pydantic: the API contract\n  repository.py    queries\n  service.py       rules: raises DomainError subclasses\n  router.py        thin routes: Depends(require(...)), Depends(get_session)\n```\n\n**Connections to the rest of the platform:**\n\n- It **installs** ports in `setup` (notifications installs `app.state.notifier`, the implementation of `notify()`).\n- It **calls** other modules' ports (`tenant_settings`, `secret_vault`, `tenant_profile`).\n- It **declares** what it needs and offers in the manifest, so a reader learns its contract without opening other files."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A kitchen appliance","md":"A module is an appliance: everything it needs is inside the case, it plugs into standard sockets (ports), and a label on the back (the manifest) tells you its power needs and what it does. You can replace it without rewiring the house."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Open up a module","md":"Explore the notifications module, find the line that isolates its table, and check the no-imports rule yourself."}},"foundation/repo-tour":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Nadia's first clone","md":"Nadia cloned SaaS-Foundation, ran `make setup && make check`, and watched several hundred tests pass against a real PostgreSQL in a few minutes. Then she opened the folder and saw dozens of directories. Rumana's advice: \"Don't read it all. Read the README, then CLAUDE.md, then the module you're touching, then its tests. In that order.\"\n\n> **About this realm:** you'll explore a *miniature* of SaaS-Foundation written for this course. It has the same layout, rules and patterns as the real repository, in simplified code. The real repository is private: you'll get access through your team."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"The layout","md":"```\napps/api/            the reference product: main.py lists the modules\napps/web/            the Next.js web app\npython/foundation-core/   the core: module system, ports, tenancy, RLS helpers, HTTP edge\nmodules/<name>/      one backend module each: tenants, auth, organizations, notifications, ...\npackages/            design-system, tokens, the generated TypeScript SDK\ndocs/                ARCHITECTURE, ROADMAP, HANDOFF, ADRs, THREAT-MODEL, intern guide + task cards\n```\n\n**Read first:** `README.md` → `CLAUDE.md` (the rules; CI enforces several) → `docs/HANDOFF.md` (current state) → `docs/intern/README.md`.\n\n**Make targets you'll use daily:** `make setup`, `make check` (everything CI runs: finish every task with it), `make test`, `make sdk`, `make dev-db`, `make api`, `make worker`."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A city map with districts","md":"`foundation-core` is the city's infrastructure (roads, power, water). Each module is a district with its own buildings and a few official connections to the infrastructure (ports). `apps/api/main.py` decides which districts this particular city has. You don't need to know every street to start working in one district."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps","md":"- Reading every file before starting (you'll forget most of it).\n- Skipping CLAUDE.md: its rules are enforced by CI, and reviewers will point you back to it.\n- Running the API against a database without following DEV-SETUP (the app refuses unsafe database roles, on purpose)."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Look around","md":"Explore the miniature repository the way you'll explore the real one on day one."}},"foundation/request-lifecycle":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Following one request","md":"Rumana's exercise for new engineers: \"Pick one request and follow it through the code until it touches the database, writing down every place that could stop it.\" It takes an hour and teaches more about the architecture than a week of reading at random."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"GET /v1/notifications/deliveries on acme.<base>","md":"1. **RequestContextMiddleware**: assigns a request id, enforces the body-size limit, adds security headers, writes the JSON access log.\n2. **CorsMiddleware**: if a browser sent an `Origin`, is it this tenant's own host or a platform origin?\n3. **TenantMiddleware**: validates the Host (`hostname[:port]` or 400), resolves it to a tenant (or 404), stores the tenant in the request context.\n4. **Global rate limits** per IP (and optionally per tenant).\n5. **Route dependencies**: `require(\"notifications.read\")` authenticates the bearer token and checks the permission; `get_session` opens a transaction and runs `set_config('app.tenant_id', <acme>, true)`.\n6. **The handler** calls the repository; PostgreSQL's **RLS policy** returns only Acme's rows (and none if no tenant was set).\n7. The **response model** shapes the JSON; the transaction commits; the request id goes back in `X-Request-ID`.\n\nEvery step can stop the request before the next one runs, which is what \"defence in depth\" looks like in code."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Airport security","md":"Check-in (request context), passport control (CORS), the gate that only boards passengers for *this* flight (tenant resolution), the boarding-pass scan (authentication and permission), and finally a seat that physically only exists on your plane (RLS). Getting through one checkpoint doesn't skip the others."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Trace it in the code","md":"Use grep to find each checkpoint in the miniature codebase."}},"foundation/tests-ci":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The test that failed on purpose","md":"A reviewer asked a contributor: \"Your PR adds a check that reset links expire. How do you know the test would catch it if someone removed the check?\" The contributor deleted the check, ran the tests, saw one fail, restored the check, and wrote \"mutation check: removing the expiry condition fails test_reset_links_expire\" in the PR. That sentence is now a required part of every security-sensitive PR."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"How the codebase protects itself","md":"- **Real-PostgreSQL tests**: the suite starts its own embedded Postgres, because RLS, roles and constraints *are* the security.\n- **Structural tests**: every tenant table has RLS enabled and forced; models match migrations; upgrading from the first revisions keeps data; the app refuses a superuser connection.\n- **Behaviour tests** through the real HTTP routes, including cross-tenant attempts.\n- **Mutation checks** for security rules: break it, see red, restore.\n- **`make check`** = tests + import contracts + typechecks + token contrast + SDK freshness.\n- **CI on every PR**: `check`, `secrets` (gitleaks over the whole history), `audit` (pip-audit and pnpm audit, blocking on high).\n- **Spec tests** for upcoming work live, skipped, in `apps/api/tests/todo/`: the intern's acceptance criteria as code."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Fire drills","md":"Smoke detectors (tests) are only trusted because someone presses the test button now and then and hears the alarm (mutation checks). CI is the building inspector who visits before every change is allowed in."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Read the guards","md":"Read an isolation test, the CI workflow and the PR template."}},"frontend/design-a11y":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"One in six","md":"About one in six people has a significant disability, and far more have temporary or situational ones: a broken arm, a toddler on one hip, glare on a phone screen, slow mobile data. When an interface works with a keyboard, a screen reader and good contrast, it works better for everyone."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Design systems","md":"A **design system** is a shared set of **tokens** (named design decisions) and **components** (built once, accessibly) that every product uses.\n\n- **Primitive tokens**: the raw palette and scales (`blue-600`, `space-4`).\n- **Semantic tokens**: meaning (`primary`, `danger`, `surface`, `text-muted`), mapped to primitives per **brand** and **light/dark mode**.\n- Components use only semantic tokens, so a new tenant brand changes colours everywhere without touching a component.\n\nSaaS-Foundation: `packages/tokens` (two brands, **contrast-checked at build time**), `packages/design-system` (Button, Input, FormField, Card, ThemeProvider on **React Aria** for keyboard and screen-reader behaviour). Tenant brand overrides are validated on the server too."},{"id":"model-2","kind":"model","title":"Accessibility (a11y) basics (WCAG AA)","md":"- **Labels** on every form control (a `<label>`, not a placeholder).\n- **Keyboard**: everything reachable with Tab, usable with Enter/Space, with a visible focus ring; no keyboard traps.\n- **Contrast**: 4.5:1 for normal text, 3:1 for large text and UI parts.\n- **Alt text** for meaningful images; `alt=\"\"` for decorative ones.\n- **Structure**: headings in order, landmarks (`<main>`, `<nav>`), real buttons and links.\n- **Don't rely on colour alone** (add icons or text to errors).\n- Respect **reduced motion**; support **200% zoom**."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Ramps and kerb cuts","md":"Kerb cuts were built for wheelchair users, and now parents with prams, travellers with suitcases and delivery workers with trolleys all use them. Accessible design is the kerb cut of software."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps","md":"- \"We'll add accessibility later\": retrofitting is far more expensive.\n- Custom widgets (dropdowns, modals) without keyboard support.\n- Brand colours chosen without a contrast check."}],"lab":null},"frontend/frontend-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Before the redesign ships","md":"A designer handed over a new signup page built quickly in plain HTML, outside the design system. Rumana: \"Review it the way a screen reader, a keyboard and a tired user on a sunny street would experience it. Then we'll rebuild it with our components.\""},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"A quick accessibility review","md":"1. Images: meaningful ones have descriptive `alt`, decorative ones `alt=\"\"`.\n2. Forms: every input has a label.\n3. Interactive things are real `<button>`s and `<a>`s.\n4. Headings are real headings, in order.\n5. Text contrast meets 4.5:1."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Review the signup page","md":"Fetch the HTML, search it, and write findings with fixes."}},"frontend/html-css":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The button nobody could press","md":"A redesign replaced every `<button>` with a nicely styled `<div>`. It looked identical. Then support calls started: keyboard users couldn't submit forms, screen-reader users heard \"Sign up\" as plain text, and mobile users in bright sunlight couldn't see the pale grey hint text. All of it came from forgetting what HTML elements *mean*."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"HTML: structure and meaning","md":"HTML describes **what things are**: `<header>`, `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<h1>`-`<h6>` (one `h1` per page, levels in order), `<p>`, `<ul>/<li>`, `<a>` (goes somewhere), `<button>` (does something), `<form>`, `<label>`, `<input>`.\n\n**Semantic elements** come with free behaviour: `<button>` is focusable, works with Enter and Space, and is announced as a button. `<label for=\"email\">` names its input for screen readers and makes the label clickable."},{"id":"model-2","kind":"model","title":"CSS: presentation","md":"- **Selectors and specificity**: element < class < id < inline style. Later rules win ties. Prefer classes; avoid `!important`.\n- **The box model**: content → padding → border → margin. Use `box-sizing: border-box`.\n- **Layout**: **flexbox** for one direction (toolbars, rows of buttons), **grid** for two (page layouts, card grids).\n- **Responsive design**: fluid widths, `rem` units, media queries: design for the phone first, then widen.\n- In SaaS-Foundation you'll mostly write **Tailwind** utility classes driven by **design tokens** (`bg-primary`, `text-muted`), not raw CSS colours."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A building's structure and its paint","md":"HTML is the structure of a building: walls, doors, stairs, signs saying which room is which. CSS is the paint, lighting and furniture. You can repaint freely, but if you draw a door on a wall instead of building one (a div styled as a button), people can't walk through it."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps","md":"- Placeholders as labels (they vanish when typing and are usually low contrast).\n- Skipping heading levels or using headings for size instead of structure.\n- Fixed pixel widths that break on small phones.\n- Clickable divs and spans."}],"lab":null},"frontend/javascript":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The menu that said \"undefined\"","md":"A bakery's menu page showed \"undefined undefined undefined\" for an afternoon. The API had started returning `{ \"items\": [...] }` instead of a plain list, and the JavaScript did `data.map(...)` on the object. Nothing crashed on the server, every test there passed, and users saw nonsense. Frontend code runs on thousands of devices you don't control, so types and careful handling of API responses matter."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"JavaScript essentials","md":"- **Values**: numbers, strings, booleans, `null`/`undefined`, objects `{}`, arrays `[]`. Use `===` (no type conversion), `const` by default, `let` when it changes.\n- **Functions** and **closures**: a function remembers the variables around it.\n- **The DOM**: the page as a tree of objects; `document.querySelector`, `addEventListener('click', ...)`. Use `textContent`, not `innerHTML`, for user data (XSS).\n- **Asynchronous code**: the browser has one thread. Slow work (network, timers) uses **promises**; `async`/`await` makes them readable:\n\n```js\nasync function loadMenu() {\n  const res = await fetch('/api/menu');\n  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`menu failed: ${res.status}`);  // fetch does NOT throw on 404/500\n  return (await res.json()).items;\n}\n```\n\n- **Event loop**: synchronous code runs first, then promise callbacks, then timers.\n- **Modules**: `import`/`export` split code into files."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A single waiter","md":"JavaScript in the browser is one waiter serving a whole restaurant. They never stand at the kitchen door waiting for one dish (that would freeze the page); they take the order, move on, and come back when the kitchen rings (a promise resolves)."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps","md":"- Assuming `fetch` throws on HTTP errors.\n- `innerHTML` with user data.\n- Forgetting `await` (you get a Promise, not the data).\n- `==` surprises (`0 == \"\"` is `true`)."}],"lab":null},"frontend/nextjs":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Fast first paint, and a secret that stayed secret","md":"A product team moved their React app to Next.js. Pages appeared immediately (HTML rendered on the server) instead of after a spinner, search engines could index them, and the API token moved out of the browser entirely into a server-side cookie. Same React skills, different place where code runs."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Where code runs","md":"- **Client-side rendering (CSR)**: the browser downloads JS and builds the page. Interactive, but slower first paint.\n- **Server-side rendering (SSR)**: HTML is generated on the server per request, then **hydrated** (made interactive) in the browser.\n- **Static generation (SSG)**: HTML built at deploy time.\n\n**Next.js (app router):**\n\n- Folders in `app/` are routes (`app/settings/page.tsx` → `/settings`); `layout.tsx` wraps pages.\n- Components are **server components** by default: they run on the server, can read secrets and call the API directly, and send no JS for themselves. Interactive components start with `\"use client\"`.\n- **Route handlers** (`app/api/.../route.ts`) are small server endpoints: perfect for a **backend-for-frontend** (BFF).\n- **Middleware** runs before every request (redirects, headers).\n- **Env vars**: only `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` reach the browser, so never prefix secrets with it."},{"id":"inside","kind":"inside","title":"SaaS-Foundation's web app","md":"`apps/web` is a Next.js app. Its middleware sets a **per-request CSP nonce** and security headers, and passes the public host to the API as `X-Tenant-Host` (honoured only from trusted proxies). The root layout asks the API which tenant this host belongs to and applies that tenant's **brand tokens**. ADR 0001 decides that sign-in goes through route handlers that keep the API token in an httpOnly `__Host-` cookie."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A restaurant with a kitchen and a table-side grill","md":"Server components are dishes prepared in the kitchen and served ready (fast, and the recipe never leaves the kitchen). Client components are the table-side grill: interactive, but you have to bring equipment (JavaScript) to the table."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps","md":"- Secrets in `NEXT_PUBLIC_` variables or client components.\n- Marking everything `\"use client\"` (you lose server rendering and ship more JS).\n- Forgetting that server components run per request: avoid slow, uncached work there."}],"lab":null},"frontend/react-typescript":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"One component, fifty screens","md":"SaaS-Foundation's design system has one `Button` component. It's used on every screen of every product built on the platform. When the accessibility review asked for a bigger focus ring, one change fixed fifty screens. That is the point of components: build a piece of UI once, correctly, and reuse it everywhere."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"React","md":"- A **component** is a function that takes **props** and returns UI written in **JSX**:\n\n```tsx\ntype Props = { title: string; done: boolean; onToggle: () => void };\n\nexport function NoteRow({ title, done, onToggle }: Props) {\n  return (\n    <li>\n      <label>\n        <input type=\"checkbox\" checked={done} onChange={onToggle} /> {title}\n      </label>\n    </li>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n- **State** (`useState`) is data a component owns; changing it re-renders. **Props** flow down; **callbacks** flow events up.\n- **Effects** (`useEffect`) synchronise with the outside world (subscriptions, timers). Data fetching usually goes through a library or a server component instead.\n- **Lists** need stable `key`s (ids, not array indexes).\n- React **escapes** values in JSX by default (XSS protection), unless you use `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` (the name is the warning)."},{"id":"model-2","kind":"model","title":"TypeScript","md":"TypeScript adds **types** to JavaScript and checks them before the code runs: typos in property names, wrong argument types and missing `null` checks become compile errors instead of user-facing bugs. SaaS-Foundation's frontend uses a **generated SDK**, so API calls are typed from the backend's OpenAPI description."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"LEGO","md":"Components are LEGO bricks: small, standard pieces with defined connectors (props). You build big things by combining them, and a better brick improves everything built with it. TypeScript is the shape of the studs: pieces that don't fit simply don't click."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps","md":"- Copying state that could be derived (two sources of truth drift apart).\n- Array-index keys.\n- `any` everywhere (it switches TypeScript off).\n- Huge components that do fetching, logic and layout at once."}],"lab":null},"hero/hero-final":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Ready for task card 00","md":"You've walked the whole map: the habits of the craft, the web and HTTP, security, frontend and backend, multi-tenancy, the SaaS-Foundation codebase, and building software with AI by your side without letting it drive. One last check, then you're ready for your first real task card."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What comes next","md":"1. **Your first task card**: `docs/intern/tasks/00-warmup-members.md` in the real SaaS-Foundation repository.\n2. **Git Conquest** (https://mosaddek2020.github.io/git-conquest/) for deeper git and GitHub.\n3. **DevOps Conquest** (https://mosaddek2020.github.io/devops-conquest/) for how software runs: Linux, networking, CI/CD, containers, Kubernetes, observability.\n4. Keep the habits: read first, ask early, small reviewed changes, tests from the spec, mutation checks for security rules, and AI that drafts while you decide."}],"lab":null},"hero/hero-incident":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Friday, 16:45","md":"Friday afternoon, Acme Bakery's support line lights up: nobody can log in. \"Too many requests\", the app says, even for people who haven't tried in days. Nothing in the application was deployed this week. Rumana is on a plane. You are on call."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Bring every realm","md":"- **HTTP**: status codes and headers tell you which part answered and why (429, `Retry-After`, request ids).\n- **Logs**: structured fields (`client_ip`, `status`) show patterns at a glance.\n- **What changed?** Code, config, infrastructure, data.\n- **Security model**: per-IP limits and lockouts rely on knowing the *real* client IP, which behind a proxy only works if the proxy is **trusted**.\n- **Fix, verify, write it up** blamelessly, with a follow-up that stops it happening again."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Handle the incident","md":"Reproduce, investigate, fix, verify, and write the postmortem."}},"hero/hero-review":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Five problems, one PR","md":"A contractor opens \"Add report search (generated with AI, tests pass)\". It's short. It also contains, in about twenty lines, a problem from almost every realm you've completed. Rumana: \"This is the review I'd want from a teammate. Find all of them, say why each matters, and give a clear verdict.\""},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Review with the whole map","md":"- **Secrets**: anything key-shaped in code.\n- **Access control**: ownership checks on every object; permissions on every route.\n- **Injection**: SQL built from strings.\n- **Scale**: lists without limits.\n- **Tests**: what was *removed* matters as much as what was added.\n- A verdict: approve, or request changes with blocking items listed."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Review feat/report-search","md":"Read the diff and write the review."}},"http/cookies-sessions":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The logout button that didn't log anyone out","md":"A team's logout button deleted the cookie in the browser and showed \"You are signed out\". But the server never forgot the session. An attacker who had copied the cookie earlier (from a shared computer) kept full access for two weeks. Logging out has to happen on the server."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Remembering users over a stateless protocol","md":"HTTP is **stateless**: each request stands alone. To stay signed in, every request must carry proof.\n\n**Cookies**: the server sends `Set-Cookie: name=value; attributes`, and the browser automatically returns `Cookie: name=value` to that site on later requests.\n\n| Attribute | Protects against |\n|---|---|\n| `HttpOnly` | JavaScript (and so XSS) reading the cookie |\n| `Secure` | sending it over plain HTTP |\n| `SameSite=Lax/Strict` | sending it on cross-site requests (CSRF) |\n| `Max-Age` / `Expires` | living forever |\n| `__Host-` prefix | being set for other subdomains (forces Secure, Path=/, no Domain) |\n\n**Server sessions vs tokens**: a session id (cookie or opaque token) is looked up on the server, so it can be **revoked** instantly (logout, password change, \"sign out everywhere\"). Self-contained tokens (like JWTs) are valid until expiry unless you add revocation. SaaS-Foundation uses **opaque tokens stored hashed** on the server, with idle and absolute timeouts and full revocation."},{"id":"inside","kind":"inside","title":"SaaS-Foundation's browser model (ADR 0001)","md":"The API speaks bearer tokens. Browsers never hold them: a Next.js server (a backend-for-frontend) keeps the token in an httpOnly, Secure, SameSite, `__Host-` cookie and calls the API on the user's behalf. JavaScript on the page can't read the token, so even an XSS bug can't steal a reusable credential."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A wristband at a festival","md":"You show your ticket once at the gate and get a wristband (the session cookie). Staff check the wristband, not your ticket, every time you enter an area. If you leave for good, a responsible festival cuts the wristband off *and* marks its number invalid at every gate (server-side logout)."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Sign in with curl","md":"Do by hand what a browser does automatically: sign in, carry the cookie, and check that logout really ends the session."}},"http/cors":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"\"Just set it to *\"","md":"A new developer hit a red CORS error in the browser console and found a forum answer: add `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`. The error went away. A security reviewer later explained what else had gone away: the browser's protection that stops any random website from reading the API's responses with the user's session."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Origins and the same-origin policy","md":"An **origin** is **scheme + host + port**: `https://acme.example.com` and `https://api.acme.example.com` are different origins, and so are `http://` and `https://` versions of the same host.\n\nThe browser's **same-origin policy**: JavaScript on a page from origin A can *send* requests to origin B, but it can't *read* the responses unless B allows it.\n\n**CORS** (cross-origin resource sharing) is how B allows it, with response headers:\n\n- `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://acme.example.com` (a specific origin, never `*` with credentials)\n- `Access-Control-Allow-Methods`, `Access-Control-Allow-Headers` (for **preflight** responses)\n- `Vary: Origin` (so caches don't mix up answers for different origins)\n\n**Preflight**: for \"non-simple\" requests (JSON bodies, custom headers like `Authorization`), the browser first sends `OPTIONS` with `Origin` and `Access-Control-Request-Method`. If the answer doesn't allow it, the real request is never sent."},{"id":"inside","kind":"inside","title":"CORS is a browser rule, not a firewall","md":"curl, scripts and attackers' servers ignore CORS entirely. CORS protects *users* (it stops malicious websites from using their browser session to read your API); it does not protect your API from direct requests. Authentication and authorisation do that.\n\nSaaS-Foundation allows an origin only if it is a configured platform origin, or a hostname of the **same tenant** the request is for, and never sends credentials headers. The operator API accepts platform origins only."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"The bouncer who checks where you came from","md":"The browser is a bouncer standing between web pages and APIs. A page from one club can shout an order across the street to another club, but the bouncer only lets the answer back in if the other club has put this club on its guest list."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Play the browser","md":"curl doesn't enforce CORS, but you can imitate exactly what a browser sends and read what the server answers."}},"http/headers":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The deploy that didn't show up","md":"Rumana deployed a new menu, but customers kept seeing the old one for five minutes. Nothing was broken: the response said `Cache-Control: public, max-age=300`, so browsers and the CDN were allowed to keep their copy for 300 seconds. Headers are small, and they control a lot."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Headers: metadata on both sides","md":"Every request and response carries **headers**: `Name: value` lines before the body.\n\n**Request headers (client → server):**\n\n- `Host`: which site (essential when one server hosts many, as with tenants).\n- `Accept`: formats the client wants back (`application/json`, `text/csv`). `Accept-Language` for languages.\n- `Content-Type`: the format of the body being sent.\n- `Authorization: Bearer <token>`: the credential. Sensitive!\n- `Cookie`: cookies the server set earlier.\n- `User-Agent`: which client; `If-None-Match`: \"I have this version (ETag), has it changed?\"\n\n**Response headers (server → client):**\n\n- `Content-Type`, `Content-Length`.\n- `Cache-Control`: `no-store` (never cache: personal data), `max-age=300` (cache for 5 minutes), `public`/`private`.\n- `ETag`: a version id for the content; with `If-None-Match` the server can answer `304 Not Modified` with no body.\n- `Set-Cookie`, `Location` (for 201 and redirects), `Retry-After` (for 429/503), `Vary` (which request headers change the response).\n- Security headers (`Strict-Transport-Security`, `Content-Security-Policy`...): Realm 5.\n\n`curl -v` shows both sides: lines starting `>` were sent, `<` were received."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"The envelope and the letter","md":"The body is the letter. Headers are everything written on the envelope: who it's for, what language it's in, how urgent, whether to keep a copy, and a stamp proving who sent it."},{"id":"inside","kind":"inside","title":"Headers in SaaS-Foundation","md":"Every API response carries `X-Request-ID` (quote it in bug reports: it finds the exact log lines), `Cache-Control: no-store` on `/v1/` responses, and strict security headers. Request ids from a trusted proxy are reused; malformed ones are replaced."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Read and steer with headers","md":"Watch the full conversation, negotiate a format, inspect caching, and make a conditional request."}},"http/http-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The integration that ran at night","md":"A team built a nightly import from a supplier API. It worked for months, then started silently importing only the first page (the supplier had grown past 50 items), and one night it hit a rate limit, crashed halfway, and left half-imported data. Every robust integration handles **auth, pagination, rate limits and errors** from day one."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"A careful API client","md":"1. Read the docs and the error messages: they tell you how to authenticate.\n2. Keep credentials out of code and history (read them from a file or environment variable).\n3. Page until there is no `next`.\n4. On `429`/`503`, wait for `Retry-After` (and back off further if it repeats).\n5. Validate what you receive; log failures without logging the key."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Pull the supplier list","md":"Build the report the purchasing team asked for: every active supplier, from every page."}},"http/methods-status":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"\"It just says 4-something\"","md":"A support ticket: \"the app shows an error when saving\". Tanvir asked one question: \"which status code?\" It was 422. That single number told him the request reached the backend, the user was signed in (not 401), allowed (not 403), and the data failed validation. He found the bug (a date sent as \"31/12\" instead of \"2026-12-31\") in five minutes. Status codes are compressed diagnoses."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Methods: what you want to do","md":"| Method | Means | Safe? | Idempotent? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `GET` | read | yes | yes |\n| `POST` | create / perform an action | no | no |\n| `PUT` | replace entirely | no | yes |\n| `PATCH` | change some fields | no | usually no |\n| `DELETE` | remove | no | yes |\n\n**Safe** = never changes anything (so crawlers and prefetchers can call it). **Idempotent** = doing it twice has the same effect as once, which makes retries safe. That's why payments and \"send email\" need extra protection (idempotency keys, deduplication) against double-clicks and retries."},{"id":"model-2","kind":"model","title":"Status codes: what happened","md":"- **2xx success**: `200 OK`, `201 Created` (+ `Location` header), `204 No Content`.\n- **3xx redirect**: `301` moved permanently, `302/307` temporarily, `304 Not Modified` (use your cache).\n- **4xx client error** (fix the request): `400` malformed, `401` not authenticated, `403` authenticated but not allowed, `404` not found, `405` wrong method (+ `Allow` header), `409` conflict (e.g. email taken), `415` wrong content type, `422` invalid data, `429` too many requests (+ `Retry-After`).\n- **5xx server error** (not the client's fault): `500` the app crashed, `502/504` a proxy couldn't reach or wait for the app, `503` temporarily unavailable."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A post office counter","md":"GET is asking \"do you have a parcel for me?\" (asking twice changes nothing). POST is handing in a new parcel (handing it in twice sends two). 401 is \"show me your ID\"; 403 is \"I see your ID, but this parcel isn't yours\"; 404 is \"no such parcel\"; 422 is \"you filled in the form wrong\"; 500 is the clerk fainting."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps","md":"- **200 with `{\"error\": true}`**: every client, cache and monitoring tool now thinks it worked.\n- Using GET for actions (\"GET /orders/42/cancel\"): a link preview bot cancels the order.\n- Returning 500 for bad input: it's the client's mistake (4xx), and 5xx pages wake people up at night.\n- Revealing too much in 404 vs 403 (e.g. \"this account exists but...\"). Sometimes 404 is the safer answer."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Talk to the Notes API","md":"A small Notes API is running. Use every method, trigger the important status codes on purpose, and read what each one tells you."}},"http/rest-json":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The endpoint that returned the whole database","md":"An early version of an internal API had `GET /orders` with no pagination. It was fine in testing with 30 orders. In production, a big tenant had 2 million orders: one call took 40 seconds, used 3 GB of memory and took the server down. \"Every list gets a limit\" became a team rule that afternoon."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"REST in practice","md":"**Resources are nouns, methods are verbs:**\n\n```\nGET    /v1/orders              list (paginated, filterable)\nPOST   /v1/orders              create\nGET    /v1/orders/42           read one\nPATCH  /v1/orders/42           change some fields\nDELETE /v1/orders/42           delete\nPOST   /v1/orders/42/cancel    an action that isn't plain CRUD\n```\n\n**JSON shapes** stay consistent: the same field names and types everywhere, ISO 8601 dates (`2026-11-01T09:30:00Z`), money as integers in minor units (poisha, cents), never floats.\n\n**Pagination**: every list has a `limit` (with a maximum) and a way to the next page (`page`/`next`, or a cursor). **Filtering** via query parameters (`?status=open`).\n\n**Errors** have a stable machine `code`, a human `message`, and a `request_id`: `{\"code\": \"email_taken\", \"message\": \"...\", \"request_id\": \"...\"}`. Clients branch on `code`, never on the message text (which may be translated).\n\n**Versioning**: additive changes (new optional fields) need no new version; breaking changes go to `/v2`.\n\n**OpenAPI** describes the whole API in a machine-readable file: documentation, validation and generated clients come from it. FastAPI produces it automatically from your Python types."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A well-run library","md":"Books (resources) have stable call numbers (URLs). You don't ask the librarian to bring every book in the building (pagination); you search by subject (filtering). When something goes wrong you get a slip with a reason code a computer can read and a sentence a human can (error body)."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"API design traps","md":"- Leaking internal ids or database errors in responses.\n- Different shapes for the same thing on different endpoints.\n- Floats for money (`0.1 + 0.2 != 0.3`).\n- Breaking changes without a new version."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Explore a REST API","md":"Use the API's own description, page through results, filter, and extract exactly the data you need with jq."}},"practices/code-review":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Two kinds of review","md":"Tanvir's first PR got one comment: \"Not how we do it.\" He spent a day guessing what was meant. His second PR, reviewed by Rumana, got five comments, each like this: *\"blocking: this query runs once per member (N+1). On a tenant with 5,000 members that's 5,000 queries. Could we load them in one `WHERE id IN (...)`? Happy to pair on it.\"* He fixed all five in an hour and learned something from each."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Reviewing well","md":"**Look for, in order:**\n\n1. **Correctness**: does it do what the ticket says? Edge cases? Error paths?\n2. **Security and data**: secrets, permissions, validation, personal data in logs, tenant isolation.\n3. **Tests**: would a test fail if this code were wrong?\n4. **Design and readability**: will the next person understand and change it?\n5. **Style**: only what tools don't already enforce.\n\n**Write comments that can be acted on**: what, where, why it matters, a suggestion, and a severity label (**blocking**, **question**, **suggestion**, **nit**). Praise good things too."},{"id":"model-2","kind":"model","title":"Being reviewed well","md":"- **Small PRs** with a description: what and why, how you tested it, what to look at closely.\n- Review your own diff first: you'll catch half the issues.\n- Assume good intent. Ask when a comment is unclear. Reply to every comment (fixed / explained / ticket created).\n- Comments are about the code, not about you, and they are the fastest way to learn the codebase."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A second pair of eyes on the climbing rope","md":"Climbers check each other's knots and harnesses before every climb, however experienced they are. Nobody is offended: the check is part of climbing safely. Code review is the partner check before code goes to production."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Review traps","md":"- **Rubber-stamping** (\"LGTM\") without reading.\n- **Bikeshedding**: twenty comments on naming, none on the missing permission check.\n- Reviewing 2,000 lines at once: attention collapses after a few hundred.\n- Approving your own code (SaaS-Foundation's interns open draft PRs and never merge their own work)."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Review a teammate's branch","md":"A contractor asks you to review a small change to the monthly report. Read it as a reviewer and write your findings."}},"practices/config-secrets-logs":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The key that cost a weekend","md":"An intern pushed a cloud API key in a config file to a public repository. Within four minutes, automated scanners had found it, and by morning someone had started 200 crypto-mining servers on the company's account. Deleting the file in the next commit didn't help: the key was in the history, and bots had already copied it."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Configuration and secrets","md":"**12-factor config**: the same code runs in every environment (your laptop, CI, staging, production). What differs between them (database URLs, API keys, feature flags) comes from the **environment**: environment variables, or a secret manager that injects them.\n\n- Commit an **`.env.example`** with placeholders: it documents what is needed.\n- Keep your real **`.env`** local and in **`.gitignore`**.\n- Required settings **fail fast**: crash at startup with a message naming the missing variable.\n- **Secrets** (passwords, API keys, private keys, tokens) never go in code, git, tickets, chat or logs. If one leaks: **rotate it immediately**, then clean up.\n\nSaaS-Foundation goes further: tenant credentials (a client's SMTP password, a payment key) live in an **encrypted vault** (AES-GCM), never in settings or plain columns."},{"id":"model-2","kind":"model","title":"Logging that helps and doesn't leak","md":"- **Levels**: `debug` (noisy detail), `info` (normal events), `warning` (unexpected but handled), `error` (something failed).\n- **Structured logs** (JSON): fields like `status`, `path`, `request_id`, `duration_ms` that machines can filter and aggregate.\n- **Request ids** connect all the log lines of one request across services.\n- **Never log**: passwords, tokens (also inside URLs and query strings), full card numbers, more personal data than you need. SaaS-Foundation's JSON logger redacts secret-looking keys, bearer tokens, `token=` values and email addresses automatically, and the access log records paths without query strings."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"House keys","md":"Configuration is the house address: different for every house, written on the outside. Secrets are the keys: you don't print them on the door, you don't photocopy them for strangers, and if you lose one, you change the lock (rotate), you don't just hope nobody finds it."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps","md":"- `git add .` picks up `.env`. Look at `git status` before every commit.\n- \"Temporary\" debug logging of a full request, headers included (the `Authorization` header is a credential).\n- Default values for secrets (`SECRET = os.environ.get(\"SECRET\", \"dev-secret\")`): production silently runs with the default.\n- Secrets pasted into AI chat windows. (More on that in the AI realm.)"}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Set up config safely, and catch a leaky log","md":"Set up your local configuration the safe way, prove git will never pick it up, then audit a log for leaked secrets."}},"practices/design-principles":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The change that touched 40 files","md":"A team wanted to switch their email provider. Email sending had been copied into the signup code, the invoice code, the password-reset code and twelve other places, each slightly different. The \"small\" change took three weeks and broke password resets for a day. A neighbouring team made the same switch in an afternoon: their email sending lived behind one interface, and only one module knew which provider was behind it."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Principles that keep change cheap","md":"**Separation of concerns**: different jobs live in different places. In a web backend: the **route** parses HTTP and returns responses; the **service** holds business rules; the **repository** talks to the database. When the rule changes, you know where to look.\n\n**High cohesion, low coupling**: things that change together live together (cohesion); parts depend on each other as little as possible (coupling). Low coupling is what makes a change local.\n\n**Single responsibility**: a class or module should have *one reason to change*.\n\n**Depend on interfaces, not implementations** (dependency inversion): code asks for \"something that can send a message\", not \"the SMTP client in module X\". The implementation is plugged in at startup.\n\n**Composition over inheritance**: combine small, focused objects instead of building deep class trees where a change to the base ripples everywhere."},{"id":"inside","kind":"inside","title":"This is literally how SaaS-Foundation is built","md":"SaaS-Foundation's first hard rule is **\"modules never import each other\"**, and CI enforces it with a tool (`import-linter`). Modules talk through **ports**: small interfaces in `foundation_core`. The auth module needs to send a password-reset email; it calls `notify(...)`, a port that the notifications module implements. Auth has no idea how email works, and notifications can be replaced without touching auth."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Wall sockets","md":"Your laptop doesn't know which power station made the electricity. It depends on an interface (the socket and its voltage), and any compliant supply can be plugged in. Hard-wiring the laptop to one power station would be high coupling."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Design traps","md":"- **The god object**: one class that does everything, that every change has to touch.\n- **Premature abstraction**: interfaces with exactly one implementation \"for the future\" add cost without benefit. Wait for the second real use.\n- **Shared mutable state** (globals) that couples everything invisibly.\n- **Inheritance for code reuse** only: if B is not truly a kind of A, compose instead."}],"lab":null},"practices/docs-decisions":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"\"Why on earth is it like this?\"","md":"A new engineer found that SaaS-Foundation's API returns bearer tokens instead of setting cookies, and was about to \"fix\" it. Then he found `docs/adr/0001-browser-session-model.md`: a one-page record explaining that browsers never hold the token (a Next.js server keeps it in an httpOnly cookie), why that protects against stolen tokens, and which alternatives were rejected. Ten minutes of reading saved a week of work and a security hole."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Documentation that earns its keep","md":"- **README**: what this is, how to run it, how to test it, where to read more. A newcomer should be running in minutes.\n- **Docstrings and comments**: the contract (inputs, units, errors) and the *why* of surprising code.\n- **ADRs (architecture decision records)**: one short page per important decision: context, decision, consequences, rejected options. They answer \"why is it like this?\" years later.\n- **Changelogs and versions**: semantic versioning `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`: breaking change, new feature, fix.\n- **Runbooks**: step-by-step instructions for operating the system (how to reset an operator's MFA, how to rotate a key).\n\nDocs change **in the same PR** as the code they describe, or they start to lie."},{"id":"model-2","kind":"model","title":"Estimates and \"done\"","md":"- Estimate in **ranges with assumptions** (\"1-3 days if the endpoint exists\"), and update early when you learn more.\n- Break big tasks into steps you can finish in a day.\n- **Definition of done**: tests pass, reviewed, docs updated, deployed or ready to deploy, ticket updated. SaaS-Foundation's PR template turns it into a checklist."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A ship's log","md":"A ship's log records course changes and the reasons for them. When something goes wrong at sea, nobody asks the captain to remember; they read the log. Code tells you what the system does; good docs and ADRs tell you why."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps","md":"- Docs that describe the *intended* system, not the real one.\n- Huge wikis nobody updates. Keep docs next to the code, small and current.\n- Estimates given to please, not to inform."}],"lab":null},"practices/practices-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Friday afternoon, a \"quick fix\"","md":"A contractor couldn't get local charging to work, so he pasted the live payment key into the code, added a print to debug it, and opened a PR titled \"Quick fix: make charging work locally\". It has one approval from someone who didn't read it. It's yours to review before it merges."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Responding to a leaked secret","md":"1. **Don't merge.** Fix the code: read the key from the environment, remove every print of it.\n2. **Rotate** the key at the provider now: it is already in the branch's history, on the server and in everyone's clones.\n3. Tell the team clearly: what leaked, where, what you did, what's left.\n4. Ask what would have caught it earlier (secret scanning in CI, a pre-commit hook), blamelessly."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Stop the merge, fix it right","md":"Review the change, fix it on its branch, and write the note the team needs."}},"saas/identity-roles":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Two bugs the review found","md":"SaaS-Foundation's own security review (November 2026) found two identity bugs that only make sense in a multi-tenant system. First, a tenant **admin** (not owner) could change the tenant's SMTP server to their own and then request a password reset for the **owner**: the reset link arrived on the admin's server. Second, switching a tenant's MFA policy to \"admins\" didn't affect admins who were already signed in. Both were fixed with tests that fail if the fix is removed."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Roles and permissions","md":"- **Operators** (platform staff): CLI-created only, mandatory TOTP MFA (an enrolment-only session until they enrol), stricter lockout, 8-hour sessions, their own append-only audit log. Any action on a tenant is written to **that tenant's** audit log as `actor_type=\"operator\"`.\n- **Tenant roles** (`tenant_admin` module): `owner` and `admin`. A tenant always keeps at least one owner.\n- **Organization roles** inside a tenant: `owner`, `admin`, `member`.\n- **Permissions** are `<module>.<verb>` strings declared in each module's manifest, granted to roles there (`tenant_grants`, `role_grants`), and checked in routes with `require(\"organizations.invite\")`."},{"id":"model-2","kind":"model","title":"Getting power safely","md":"- **First owner**: an operator issues a single-use, expiring **claim**; the first admin registers, **verifies their email**, and redeems it.\n- **Invites** (organizations and tenant admins): hashed, single-use, 7 days, bound to the **verified** invited email, rate-limited per inviter.\n- **MFA policy** per tenant (`off`, `admins`, `everyone`), checked on every request.\n- **Step-up**: settings writes, secrets and admin-role changes need an MFA check from the last 10 minutes.\n- **Owner-only settings**: mail routing, credentials, MFA policy, OAuth configuration.\n- **Sensitive emails** (links, security notices) can't be reworded by tenants and always use the platform's mail server."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Keys in a hotel chain","md":"Hotel staff (operators) have master keys, but every use is logged and the guest is told. Each hotel's manager (tenant owner) controls that hotel only, and can make deputies (admins), but deputies can't change where the manager's mail is delivered."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps","md":"- Checking role names (`if role == \"admin\"`) instead of permissions.\n- Admin features without MFA.\n- Anything that lets a lower role redirect or read a higher role's credentials or links."}],"lab":null},"saas/modular-monolith":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Swapping the engine while driving","md":"A product built on SaaS-Foundation needed to replace its email provider with SMS for some messages. Because `auth` only calls the `notify()` port and never imports the notifications module, the team could add a new channel inside notifications without touching auth, invites, or any other caller. The change was one module, one PR."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"A monolith made of modules","md":"SaaS-Foundation is a **modular monolith**: one deployable app built from independent modules.\n\n- **A product is a list of modules**: `create_app([tenants, auth, organizations, entitlements, jobs, audit, settings, operator, tenant_admin, notifications, ...])`.\n- Each module has a **manifest** (`define_module(...)`): name, `depends_on`, permissions and grants, migrations (its own Alembic branch), templates, settings, owner-only keys, maintenance, events.\n- **Modules never import each other.** They communicate through:\n  - **Ports** in `foundation_core` (`notify`, `secret_vault`, `tenant_settings`, `user_directory`, `require_feature`, `rate_limiter`...): one module installs the implementation at startup, others call it.\n  - **Events**: `events.publish(...)` (in-process, best effort) or `events.publish_durable(session, ...)` (the outbox: retried, never lost).\n- **`foundation_core` knows no module.**\n- CI enforces the boundaries with **import-linter** (Python) and **dependency-cruiser** (TypeScript).\n\n**Why not microservices?** Separate services add network failures, distributed transactions, many deploys and harder debugging. Clear module boundaries give most of the independence today, and are exactly where you'd split later if a measured need appears."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A stereo system with standard plugs","md":"Speakers, amplifier and turntable are separate boxes that connect through standard plugs (ports). You can replace the speakers without opening the amplifier. A monolith without modules is a stereo where everything is soldered together."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps","md":"- \"Just this once\" importing another module's internals.\n- Ports that grow to expose everything (keep them small).\n- Putting module-specific logic into `foundation_core`."}],"lab":null},"saas/row-level-security":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The missing WHERE that leaked nothing","md":"During a code review, Rumana spotted a new query: `select(Invoice).where(Invoice.status == \"open\")`. No tenant filter. In many systems that's a data breach waiting for a deploy. Here, the test that exercised it returned only the current tenant's invoices: PostgreSQL's row-level security had added the tenant filter itself. The missing filter was still fixed (the repository adds one too), but the safety net had held."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"How RLS works","md":"Every tenant table gets, in its migration:\n\n```sql\nALTER TABLE invoices ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;\nALTER TABLE invoices FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;             -- even the table owner is subject to it\nCREATE POLICY tenant_isolation ON invoices\n  USING      (tenant_id = NULLIF(current_setting('app.tenant_id', true), '')::uuid)\n  WITH CHECK (tenant_id = NULLIF(current_setting('app.tenant_id', true), '')::uuid);\n```\n\n- At the start of each transaction, the app runs `set_config('app.tenant_id', '<tenant>', true)`: valid for that transaction only.\n- `USING` filters what you can **read, update and delete**; `WITH CHECK` stops you **writing** rows for another tenant.\n- **Fail closed**: if no tenant was set, the setting is empty, `NULLIF` makes it NULL, and *no row matches*. Bugs show up as empty results, never as other tenants' data.\n- The app connects as a **runtime role** that is **not superuser and not BYPASSRLS** (those skip policies); it refuses to start otherwise. Migrations run as a separate **owner** role.\n\nIn SaaS-Foundation code: models mix in `TenantScoped`, migrations call `enable_rls(\"<table>\")`, and `get_session` gives every route a session bound to the request's tenant."},{"id":"inside","kind":"inside","title":"The tests that keep it true","md":"- `test_every_tenant_table_enforces_rls`: every `TenantScoped` table has RLS **enabled and forced** with a `tenant_isolation` policy.\n- `test_startup_guard_rejects_superuser`.\n- HTTP tests creating two tenants and checking that nothing crosses.\n- Housekeeping visits each tenant through its own scoped session (`for_each_tenant`), so even cleanup never needs a bypass."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Glasses that only show your own name","md":"Imagine every database row is written in ink that only shows up through special glasses, and the glasses are cut for one tenant at the start of each transaction. Even if your code asks for \"all invoices\", you can only see your tenant's. Forget to put the glasses on, and you see nothing at all."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps","md":"- Connecting the app as a superuser \"just locally\": the startup guard stops you, and that's on purpose.\n- Tables with tenant data that forget `TenantScoped`/`enable_rls` (the test catches it).\n- Caches, files or search indexes outside the database that don't include the tenant in their keys: RLS can't protect those."}],"lab":null},"saas/saas-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The security questionnaire","md":"A large client sends a 200-question security questionnaire before signing. Question 47: \"Describe how you prevent other customers from accessing our data, and provide evidence.\" Rumana: \"Let's not answer with adjectives. Run the tests yourself and write down exactly what you sent and what came back.\""},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Defence in depth, verified","md":"- **Edge**: the tenant comes from a validated host; client-supplied tenant headers are ignored unless from trusted proxies.\n- **Application**: sessions and tokens are bound to their tenant; permissions per route.\n- **Database**: RLS filters every query and fails closed.\n- **Evidence**: requests and responses, plus the CI tests that check the same properties on every change."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Test isolation from the outside","md":"Use Acme's token against Acme and Globex, try to choose a tenant with a header, and write the evidence."}},"saas/tenancy-models":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The header that chose your tenant","md":"Early versions of many SaaS apps decided the tenant from a header the frontend sent, like `X-Tenant: acme`. It worked perfectly, until someone opened their browser's tools, changed the header to another company's name, and was looking at that company's dashboard. Which tenant a request belongs to is a security decision, and it must come from something the client can't freely choose, or that is verified."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Tenancy models","md":"| Model | What is shared | Trade-off |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Silo** | nothing: servers and database per tenant | strongest isolation, most expensive to run and upgrade |\n| **Bridge** | app shared, database (or schema) per tenant | in between |\n| **Pool** | app and database shared; every row has `tenant_id` | cheapest, simplest upgrades; isolation must be enforced rigorously |\n\nSaaS-Foundation uses the **pool** model, with isolation enforced in the database (next lesson). A dedicated-instance option for clients who demand it is on the roadmap."},{"id":"model-2","kind":"model","title":"Resolving the tenant","md":"1. Read the request's **host**: `acme.foundation.example.com`, or a **verified custom domain** like `shop.acme.com`.\n2. Validate it is a clean `hostname[:port]` (anything else: **400**). This fix came from a real security review: a crafted Host header once made password-reset links point to an attacker's site.\n3. Look it up: unknown host → **404 unknown_tenant**.\n4. Put the tenant in the request **context**; every database session for this request is bound to it.\n\nBehind a proxy (the Next.js server), the proxy passes the public host as `X-Tenant-Host`, which is only honoured when it comes from **trusted proxies**. The app refuses to start if you enable the header without listing them.\n\n**Noisy neighbours**: one tenant's heavy usage mustn't slow the others: rate limits, plan quotas and per-tenant limits."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Mail sorting by address","md":"The building's mailroom sorts letters by the address on the envelope (the host), not by a note inside saying \"please give this to apartment 12\" (a client-chosen header). And a letter with a smudged, impossible address is returned, not guessed at."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"One address, many tenants","md":"A miniature SaaS-Foundation edge is running. See how the host decides the tenant, and how it refuses bad hosts."}},"saas/what-is-saas":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"One codebase, three hundred bakeries","md":"A software company built an ordering system for one bakery. Then a second bakery wanted it, then a tenth. Each got its own copy, servers and database. By bakery fifty, every bug fix meant fifty deploys, half the copies were years out of date, and nobody knew which bakery ran which version. They rebuilt it as **one** platform serving every bakery as a **tenant**: one deploy, one version, upgrades for everyone at once. That is SaaS, and it is what SaaS-Foundation is for."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"The SaaS model","md":"- **Software as a service**: the provider runs the software; customers use it over the internet, usually by subscription.\n- **Tenants**: the customer companies. Each has its own users, data, settings, brand and (optionally) domain, and shares the platform with the others.\n- **Plans and entitlements**: what a tenant's plan includes (`custom_domain`, `files` up to 5 GB). Code checks `require_feature(app, session, \"files\")`; billing writes entitlements.\n- **White-labelling**: each tenant's users see the tenant's brand. In SaaS-Foundation, brands are token overrides, checked for contrast on the server.\n\n**Three kinds of people:**\n\n| Who | What they do | In SaaS-Foundation |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Operators** | platform staff: create tenants, grant plans, support | separate accounts, CLI-created, **mandatory MFA**, own audit log |\n| **Tenant admins** | a tenant's owners and admins: settings, secrets, other admins | `tenant_admin` module, owner-only security settings |\n| **Users** | the tenant's staff and customers | accounts **per tenant**: the same email at two tenants is two accounts |"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"An apartment building","md":"The platform is the building: one structure, shared plumbing and electricity, maintained by building staff (operators). Each tenant rents an apartment with its own lock, furniture and name on the door. Building staff can enter in an emergency, but it's logged and the tenant is told. And no tenant's key must ever open a neighbour's door."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps for a SaaS engineer","md":"- Forgetting the tenant in any query, cache key, file path or log line.\n- Looking users up across tenants.\n- Hard-coding one tenant's needs into the platform instead of making it a setting or entitlement."}],"lab":null},"security/authn-authz":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Signed in is not the same as allowed","md":"An app checked carefully that every request came from a signed-in user. It did not check *which* user. Any customer could open `/invoices/1`, `/invoices/2`, ... and read every other customer's invoices. The login system was excellent; the authorisation was missing."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Authentication: who are you?","md":"- **Passwords** are stored as slow, salted hashes designed for passwords (**Argon2id**, bcrypt), never encrypted or plain. Check new passwords against breach lists; require length over complexity rules.\n- **Rate limiting and lockout** on login; identical answers for \"no such user\" and \"wrong password\" (no **account enumeration**).\n- **MFA** (multi-factor): something you know (password) + something you have (a TOTP code from an authenticator app, a security key). Stops most account takeovers.\n- **Sessions or tokens** after login (HTTP realm), revocable, with idle and absolute timeouts.\n- **OAuth 2.0 / OpenID Connect**: \"Sign in with Google\". OIDC adds an **ID token** saying who the user is. Done right it needs `state` (bound to the browser), **PKCE**, a `nonce`, and full verification of the token's signature, issuer, audience and expiry."},{"id":"model-2","kind":"model","title":"Authorisation: what may you do?","md":"- **Roles and permissions**: e.g. `owner`, `admin`, `member`, each granting named permissions like `organizations.invite`. Check permissions, not role names, in code.\n- **Least privilege**: users, services, API keys, database roles and AI agents get only what they need.\n- **Object-level checks**: not just \"may this user read invoices?\" but \"is *this* invoice theirs (and their tenant's)?\".\n- **Step-up**: sensitive actions (changing secrets, granting admin) need a recent second-factor check."},{"id":"inside","kind":"inside","title":"How SaaS-Foundation does it","md":"- Argon2id passwords with rehash on login, a breached-password check, progressive lockout per account and IP.\n- TOTP MFA with single-use recovery codes, **mandatory for operators**, optional or required per tenant policy.\n- Permissions `<module>.<verb>` declared in each module's manifest and checked with `require(\"...\")`.\n- **Tenant isolation in the database**: PostgreSQL row-level security makes every query see only the current tenant's rows, even if the code forgets a `WHERE`."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A hotel","md":"Authentication is the front desk checking your passport and giving you a key card. Authorisation is the key card opening *your* room, the gym and the breakfast hall, and not other guests' rooms or the manager's office. A hotel with a perfect front desk and key cards that open every door is not secure."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps","md":"- Checking authorisation in the frontend only (hiding a button is not access control).\n- Role checks scattered as `if user.role == \"admin\"` instead of named permissions.\n- Admin features without MFA.\n- Forgetting the object-level check (the invoice story)."}],"lab":null},"security/https-tls":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The coffee-shop Wi-Fi","md":"In 2010 a browser extension called Firesheep let anyone on a café's Wi-Fi click a button and take over other customers' Facebook and Twitter sessions: those sites only used HTTPS for the login page, and session cookies travelled in plain text. Within months, the big sites moved everything to HTTPS. Today, plain HTTP for anything with a login is simply a vulnerability."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"HTTP vs HTTPS","md":"**HTTP** sends everything (URLs, headers, cookies, passwords, bodies) as readable text. Anyone between you and the server (Wi-Fi owner, ISP, a compromised router) can read *and change* it.\n\n**HTTPS** is HTTP inside **TLS**, which gives three guarantees:\n\n1. **Confidentiality**: the traffic is encrypted.\n2. **Integrity**: tampering is detected.\n3. **Server authentication**: the server proves it is really `acme.example.com`.\n\nIt does **not** prove the site is honest, or that its code has no bugs.\n\n**Certificates**: the server presents a certificate saying \"this key belongs to acme.example.com\", signed by a **certificate authority** (CA) the client trusts. Clients check three things:\n\n| Check | Fails as |\n|---|---|\n| Trust: signed by a known CA (chain) | \"self-signed certificate\", \"unable to get local issuer\" |\n| Time: within notBefore/notAfter | \"certificate has expired\" |\n| Name: matches the host you asked for | \"no alternative certificate subject name matches\" |\n\nLet's Encrypt issues free 90-day certificates automatically; expiring certificates are one of the most common causes of outages, so renewal must be automated and monitored."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A passport at the border","md":"The certificate is a passport. The CA is the government that issued it. The border officer (your browser) checks that the passport is from a recognised government (trust), not expired (time) and that the photo matches the person in front of them (name). `curl -k` is the officer waving everyone through without looking."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps","md":"- **`curl -k` / `verify=False` in code.** It \"fixes\" the error by turning off the protection. Fix the certificate.\n- **Mixed content**: an HTTPS page loading scripts over HTTP: the browser blocks them (or worse, an attacker swaps them).\n- **Secrets in URLs**: TLS hides the path on the wire, but servers, proxies and browser history still log it.\n- Forgetting renewal monitoring."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Four certificates, three ways to fail","md":"Four sites run HTTPS. Only one is healthy. Find out exactly what is wrong with each of the others."}},"security/owasp":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The same ten mistakes","md":"The OWASP Top 10 is a list of the most common, most damaging web vulnerabilities, updated every few years from real breach data. What's striking is how little it changes: the same families of mistakes show up decade after decade, because each new team has to learn them. You'll learn them now, from the defender's side."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"The classics and their standard defences","md":"**Broken access control** (#1): users reach data or actions they shouldn't (changing an id in the URL: **IDOR**). *Defence:* check ownership on every object access; deny by default; prefer 404 for \"not yours\"; in multi-tenant systems, enforce isolation in the database too (RLS).\n\n**Injection** (SQL, shell, template): untrusted input becomes code. *Defence:* parameterised queries / ORM (never build SQL with string concatenation), no `shell=True` with input, templates that can't execute code.\n\n**Cross-site scripting (XSS)**: input rendered as HTML/JS in other users' browsers. *Defence:* escape by default (React does; `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` doesn't), strict CSP, httpOnly cookies.\n\n**CSRF**: a malicious site makes your browser send a state-changing request with your cookies. *Defence:* SameSite cookies, Origin checks, no state changes on GET.\n\n**SSRF**: your server fetches a user-supplied URL and is tricked into calling internal services (cloud metadata, admin panels). *Defence:* allow-lists, refuse private IPs, pin the resolved IP (stop DNS rebinding), no redirects.\n\n**Security misconfiguration**: debug mode on, default passwords, verbose errors, missing headers, open storage buckets.\n\n**Vulnerable and outdated components**: dependencies with known CVEs. *Defence:* Dependabot, `pip-audit`/`npm audit` in CI.\n\n**Cryptographic failures, identification failures, logging failures**: covered across this realm and the Practices realm."},{"id":"inside","kind":"inside","title":"Evidence from SaaS-Foundation's own security review","md":"The project's November 2026 review (docs/SECURITY-REVIEW-2026-11-01.md) found, among others: a **Host-header** trick that made password-reset emails link to an attacker's site (input validation), **unlimited MFA guessing** by re-entering the password (a counter reset in the wrong place), and an **email-change link that survived a password reset**. All fixed, each with a regression test that fails if the fix is removed. Read it: it's the best lesson in this realm."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Locks, alarms and keys","md":"Access control is the locks on each door, not just the front door. Injection is a form where writing \"and open the safe\" in the name field actually opens the safe. XSS is a visitor leaving a note on the noticeboard that hypnotises the next person who reads it. SSRF is convincing the receptionist to phone the vault from inside the building."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Test your own API for IDOR","md":"Security testing your own application is part of the job. A billing API is about to ship: check that one customer can't read another's invoice."}},"security/security-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Launch on Monday","md":"A tenant's new storefront launches on Monday with a marketing campaign. Rumana asks you for a quick pre-launch security audit: \"Not a penetration test. Check the basics a customer, a browser and an auditor would check, and give me a prioritised list.\""},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"A basic external audit","md":"1. **TLS**: valid, trusted, correct name, and *how long until it expires* (is renewal automatic?).\n2. **Redirects**: does `http://` go to `https://` (301)?\n3. **Headers**: HSTS, CSP, frame protection, nosniff, referrer policy; no version leaks.\n4. **Cookies**: `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, `SameSite` on session cookies.\n5. **Report**: findings with severity, impact and fix, most urgent first."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Audit store.example.com","md":"Gather the evidence with curl and openssl, then write the findings."}},"security/security-headers":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"One missing header","md":"A bank's customer portal was embedded invisibly inside an attacker's \"win a prize\" page. Users thought they were clicking \"claim prize\"; they were clicking \"confirm transfer\" in the portal underneath (**clickjacking**). One response header (`X-Frame-Options: DENY`, or the CSP `frame-ancestors` directive) would have stopped the browser from loading the portal in a frame at all."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Headers that make browsers defend your users","md":"| Header | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| `Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains` (HSTS) | browser uses HTTPS only for this site, even for typed `http://` links |\n| `Content-Security-Policy` (CSP) | which scripts, styles, frames... may load; a strict CSP with nonces stops most injected scripts (XSS) from running |\n| `X-Frame-Options: DENY` / CSP `frame-ancestors 'none'` | no embedding in frames (clickjacking) |\n| `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` | browser trusts your `Content-Type`, doesn't guess |\n| `Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin` | full URLs (with ids, tokens) don't leak to other sites |\n| Cookie flags `HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite` | see the HTTP realm |\n\nPlus: **redirect HTTP to HTTPS** (301), and **don't advertise versions** (`Server`, `X-Powered-By`)."},{"id":"inside","kind":"inside","title":"SaaS-Foundation's headers","md":"Every API response gets `Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'` (it's JSON: nothing should ever execute), HSTS, `nosniff`, `no-store` caching, and a request id. The Next.js web app generates a fresh **nonce** per request and only scripts carrying it may run (`'strict-dynamic'` lets them load their chunks); this was verified in a real build: every Next script carried the nonce."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Instructions on the delivery box","md":"Security headers are instructions printed on the box for the courier and the recipient: \"keep upright\", \"do not leave with neighbours\", \"only open with ID\". The courier (browser) follows them faithfully, but only if they're printed on every box."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Compare two sites","md":"One site is configured carefully, one is not. Read both and write down what the weak one should change."}},"web/client-server":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"One click, four machines","md":"An Acme Bakery customer taps \"Menu\" on her phone. Her **browser** (the client) asks a **web server** for a page. The server's **backend** code checks which bakery this is, asks the **database** for today's items, and answers. The browser draws the page and runs a little **JavaScript** that asks an **API** for prices. Four machines, a dozen messages, under a second. Every web app you will work on is a variation of this conversation."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"The client-server conversation","md":"- A **client** sends a **request**; a **server** sends back a **response**. The client always speaks first.\n- **Frontend**: the code that runs on the user's device (HTML for structure, CSS for looks, JavaScript for behaviour). In SaaS-Foundation: `apps/web` (Next.js and React).\n- **Backend**: code on servers you control: authentication, permissions, validation, business rules, talking to the database. In SaaS-Foundation: the FastAPI modules in `modules/`.\n- **Database**: where data lives (PostgreSQL in SaaS-Foundation). Only the backend talks to it.\n- **API** (application programming interface): the backend's menu of requests it accepts, usually returning **JSON**.\n\n```\nbrowser  ──HTTP──>  web server / backend  ──SQL──>  database\n   ↑  HTML, CSS, JS, JSON  │\n   └───────────────────────┘\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A restaurant","md":"The customer (client) never walks into the kitchen. She orders from a waiter (the API) using a menu (the API's documentation). The kitchen (backend) decides what's possible, checks the order and cooks from the pantry (database). The plate that arrives (the response) is all the customer sees."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Who owns what","md":"On many teams, frontend and backend engineers specialise but share the **API contract**: the requests, responses and error codes both sides agree on. SaaS-Foundation generates a typed **SDK** from the backend's OpenAPI description, so the frontend can't call an endpoint that doesn't exist or misread a field."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps","md":"- **Trusting the client.** Anything the browser sends can be faked. Prices, permissions and validation are enforced on the backend, always.\n- Putting secrets in frontend code: everything sent to the browser is public."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Be the browser","md":"Use `curl` to make the same requests a browser makes when it opens the bakery's site."}},"web/ip-ports":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"\"The database is down!\" (it wasn't)","md":"A new engineer couldn't connect to the database and announced an outage. The database was fine: it only listened on `localhost`, so it was reachable from its own server and nowhere else (exactly as intended). The error said \"Connection refused\": the machine answered, but nothing was listening for *him* on that address and port."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Addresses, ports and TCP","md":"- An **IP address** identifies a machine on a network: `10.0.0.11`, or `127.0.0.1` (**localhost**: the machine itself).\n- A **port** (0-65535) identifies a program on that machine. Well-known ports: **22** SSH, **53** DNS, **80** HTTP, **443** HTTPS, **5432** PostgreSQL.\n- A program **listens** (binds) on an address and port; a client **connects** to it.\n- **TCP** gives reliable, ordered delivery (HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 run on it). **UDP** just sends packets (DNS, video calls, and HTTP/3's QUIC).\n\n**Reading connection errors:**\n\n| Error | Means |\n|---|---|\n| Could not resolve host | DNS: no address for that name |\n| Connection refused | the host answered, but nothing listens on that port (for you) |\n| Timed out | no answer: firewall, wrong address, host down |\n| An HTTP status (404, 500...) | networking worked; the application answered |"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"An office building","md":"The IP address is the building's street address. Ports are the office numbers inside. \"Refused\" is a receptionist saying \"nobody works in office 5432\"; a timeout is a building with the doors locked and nobody answering at all."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Traps","md":"- Binding a dev server to `127.0.0.1` and wondering why a teammate can't reach it (or, worse, binding a database to `0.0.0.0` on the public internet).\n- Two programs on the same port: \"address already in use\".\n- Mixing up the port in the URL (`:8080`) with the server's actual listening port."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Who is listening?","md":"Look at your own addresses, probe ports on the shop server, and see how the port in a URL changes where you land."}},"web/typing-a-url":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The interview question that isn't about memorisation","md":"\"What happens when you type a URL and press Enter?\" is a classic interview question, not because anyone needs the trivia, but because the answer is a map of everything that can go wrong. Engineers who can walk the map can debug almost any web problem."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"The journey, step by step","md":"1. **Parse the URL** and check caches (maybe the page is already cached).\n2. **DNS**: find the IP for the host (browser, OS and resolver caches first).\n3. **TCP**: open a connection to the IP and port (443 for HTTPS).\n4. **TLS handshake** (HTTPS): the server presents a **certificate** proving it is `acme.example.com`; both sides agree on encryption keys. (Realm 5 goes deep.)\n5. **HTTP request**: `GET / HTTP/1.1` plus **headers** (`Host`, `Accept`, `Cookie`...). (Realm 4 goes deep.)\n6. **Server work**: routing, authentication, business logic, database queries, maybe calls to other services.\n7. **HTTP response**: a status (`200 OK`), headers (`Content-Type`, `Cache-Control`, `Set-Cookie`...) and a body.\n8. **Rendering**: the browser parses HTML, fetches CSS, JS and images (more requests), runs JavaScript (which may call APIs), lays out and paints the page.\n\nCaches (browser, **CDN** edge servers, DNS) can answer at several points, which is why a deploy sometimes \"doesn't show up\" until caches expire."},{"id":"inside","kind":"inside","title":"Your best friend: the browser's DevTools","md":"Press F12. The **Network** tab shows every request, its status, timing, headers and response. The **Console** shows JavaScript errors. Most frontend bugs are diagnosed here in minutes."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Sending a letter","md":"Look up the address (DNS), drive to the building (TCP), the receptionist checks your ID and you check theirs (TLS), you hand over a request form (HTTP request), a clerk processes it (server work) and hands back an envelope (response), and you assemble the documents inside (rendering)."}],"lab":null},"web/urls-dns":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The typo that \"broke\" the internet","md":"A support ticket said the docs site was down. It wasn't: the ticket linked to `docs.examples.com` (with an extra \"s\"), a name that doesn't exist. Five minutes with `dig` saved an hour of restarting servers that were fine."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Anatomy of a URL","md":"```\nhttps://acme.example.com:8443/v1/orders?status=open#top\n└─┬─┘   └──────┬───────┘ └┬─┘└───┬───┘ └────┬────┘ └┬┘\nscheme       host       port  path      query   fragment\n```\n\n- **scheme**: `http` or `https` (also the default port: 80 or 443).\n- **host**: a name (looked up in DNS) or an IP address.\n- **path**: which resource on that server.\n- **query**: extra parameters (`key=value&key2=value2`). Never put secrets here: query strings end up in logs and browser history.\n- **fragment**: a place inside the page. It is *not* sent to the server."},{"id":"model-2","kind":"model","title":"DNS: names to addresses","md":"Computers connect to **IP addresses** (`203.0.113.10`); people remember **names**. **DNS** (the Domain Name System) translates. Your machine checks `/etc/hosts` and its caches, then asks a DNS resolver, which asks the servers responsible for `example.com`.\n\n- An **A record** maps a name to an IPv4 address (AAAA for IPv6); a **CNAME** points one name at another.\n- Records are **cached** for their TTL, which is why DNS changes take time to spread.\n- `dig +short name` asks DNS directly."},{"id":"inside","kind":"inside","title":"Why the host name matters in multi-tenant apps","md":"In SaaS-Foundation, `acme.example.com` and `globex.example.com` can point to the same servers. The backend reads the **host** of each request to decide which **tenant** it is for, and a security fix in 2026 made sure that host must be a clean `hostname[:port]`: a crafted Host header once made password-reset emails link to an attacker's site. Host names are input like any other."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A phone book and extensions","md":"DNS is the phone book: it turns \"Acme Bakery\" into a number. The IP address is the building's number; the port (next lesson) is the extension; the path is which document to fetch once someone picks up."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Names and addresses","md":"Look up names, reach the same server by name and by address, and see what failure looks like when a name doesn't exist."}},"web/web-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Three tickets before coffee","md":"Support has escalated three sites as \"down\". Rumana: \"Before anyone restarts anything, tell me *which layer* is broken for each one. The fix is completely different for each.\""},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Debug by layer","md":"1. **Name**: does it resolve? (`dig`, curl exit code 6)\n2. **Connection**: does a TCP connection open? (`nc -zv host port`; \"refused\" vs timeout)\n3. **HTTP**: what status and body come back? (`curl -i`)\n4. **Application**: errors in the body and logs (request ids connect the two)."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Diagnose and report","md":"Investigate all three, then write a report the right teams can act on immediately."}}};
